window.harnessChunks["rows-6664bbb425af78c7.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T19:14:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Micro</a><small>2015</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro/tree/792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\">792b18c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/task state through a model → tool/service/delegation → observation loop until an operational result is produced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, changing service/tool availability, tool/RPC/A2A results, persistent conversation state, provider responses, tool failures and intermediate observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next service/custom/built-in tool, decide whether to plan or delegate, consume returned observations, continue the loop or settle the answer. - Decision owner: the active Go Micro LLM agent in NewAgent or micro chat direct-service mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model adapters, service registry/tool discovery, built-in plan/delegate/request-input tools, memory/store, tool wrappers, step/loop/spend limits, approvals, checkpointing, retry/timeout logic, RPC/A2A/MCP integration. - Closure path: user/assigned task + current context → agent selects action/tool/delegation → Go Micro executes/gates it → tool/service/sub-agent result returns into model context → agent selects another action or final result. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent and micro chat are shipped, documented first-party runtime modes; the positive S1 loop is implemented in agent/ and CLI runtime code rather than inferred from examples or repository dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven agent while retaining registry, memory, tool execution and guardrails leaves no contextual actor choosing the next operational action. Deterministic wrappers constrain and transport the model&#39;s decisions rather than replacing them. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, cmd/micro/chat/chat.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external model inference and remote A2A peers are substrate/environment. The positive state is established independently by the first-party agent loop around those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific loop was established that regulates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct Go Micro S1 agent units at the declared product-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included multiple registered agents, RPC/A2A handoffs, service discovery/load balancing, durable flows, duplicate delegate/retry protection and shared registry/store infrastructure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific decision right is supplied. Delegation selects another actor for a subtask; service discovery/load balancing routes calls; idempotency/retry controls duplicate side effects; flow edges order work. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the reviewed standard product-runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry, client load balancing, broker/store, plan/delegate tools, A2A/MCP transport, flow sequencing, retry/idempotency guards and isolated ephemeral sub-agent context. - Closure path: no first-party path was found that detects a specific interaction disturbance among two distinct S1 agents, selects an attenuation response for that disturbance, and feeds the coordination result back into subsequent local S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multi-agent reachability, routing and delegation are present, but the Methodology explicitly requires more than communication/handoff. The reviewed product runtime does not supply an S2-specific autonomous or constructor path tied to a concrete inter-S1 oscillation/conflict witness. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, gateway/a2a/a2a.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function was established beyond local agent planning/delegation and deterministic runtime enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate current-control paths inspected included store-backed plans, delegation, flow execution state, registry discovery, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits and runtime stop/resume mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the ordinary agent may revise its own task plan and delegate subtasks, but no separate product-runtime path was found with a whole-system current view plus authority to bargain/regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities on behalf of multiple operational units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan memory, delegate routing, flow state/checkpoints, registry/client machinery, tool/step/loop/spend limits, approvals and cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: no S3-specific whole-system feedback/intervention loop was established. Local plan updates return to the same agent&#39;s task execution; deterministic limits enforce preselected constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent that decomposes one user request and delegates one subtask is not automatically S3. The reviewed evidence does not show a standard mode where an agent receives a whole-system operational view and owns ongoing resource/commitment intervention across independent S1s. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, README.md, flow/. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: .github/loop has project-level planning/current-work behavior, but it belongs to the excluded repository-development organization and is not borrowed into the product-runtime S3 mapping.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a flow-step result through a distinct verification judgment and return rejection feedback into another operational attempt before the flow proceeds with the bounded result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but deficient step outputs that ordinary body execution would otherwise return without a separate grade-and-correction pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Grader decides pass/fail and supplies corrective feedback; Verify threads rejection feedback into the next body attempt and records the final verification outcome. - Decision owner: Go Micro does not ship a sufficiently independent default autonomous auditor for the general case. LLMGrader reuses the flow model and only sees the latest result. The application must compose a grader with independence appropriate to the audited claim; Go Micro supplies the S3-specific verification/feedback closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Verify attempt loop, feedback-field injection, optional backoff, verification metadata, flow checkpoint/result state and optional LLMGrader helper. - Closure path: body produces candidate result → composed grader audits it → rejection/feedback enters the next body input → body retries → accepted or bounded-failure result becomes the flow state. - Claim being audited: whether the current flow-step output satisfies the deployment-defined rubric/ground truth before it is accepted as the step&#39;s result. - Ordinary reporting path: the body step returns its State as normal flow output. - Complementary access path: the first-party Grader seam invokes a separately composed grading actor after body completion; the deployment can give that actor external ground truth, raw artifacts or a distinct model/service, and Go Micro closes its verdict back into retry. - Independence boundary: independence is not guaranteed by the built-in LLMGrader; it is intentionally left to the developer-supplied Grader. This is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: flow.Verify deterministically reinjects grader feedback into the next body attempt; the operational body/agent then produces the corrected candidate. - Boundary reachability: flow.Verify, the Grader contract and feedback/retry closure are shipped public first-party flow primitives at the reviewed revision, not CI-only tests. A deployment only needs to supply the independent grading actor/ground-truth access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit path and return loop are first-party, but the decisive sufficiently independent audit actor is not supplied in a standard autonomous mode. General custom-code extensibility is not the basis; the credit is for the dedicated Verify/Grader primitive whose semantics are verification judgment plus corrective return. - Evidence: flow/verify.go, flow/verifytest.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: same-model LLMGrader alone is not treated as sufficient complementary independence. A deployment that wires only that helper may not operationally realize S3 even though the first-party constructor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: detect that current operational capability cannot satisfy an external requirement, develop a service-level adaptation, materialize it, and return the new capability into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user needs not covered by existing registered services/tools; requests to extend or modify an existing system; design/code/compile failures encountered while materializing the capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base mode — the micro chat agent decides that no existing service can handle the request and chooses microgenerateservice; the generation models choose the service architecture/handler changes. Parent mode — the user reviews the LLM-designed system proposal and decides at Generate? [Y/n] whether the adaptation should be materialized. - Decision owner: Base mode — the autonomous chat/generation model path. Parent mode — the human operator owns the decisive accept/reject adaptation judgment, while the generator autonomously develops and implements the approved option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry/tool discovery, generate.Design, existing-service scan, deterministic service-structure generation, LLM handler generation, bounded compile-fix loop, process startup, registry wait and tool refresh. - Closure path: external capability request + current service inventory → missing-capability distinction → model designs service adaptation → autonomous choice or parent approval triggers generation → code is written/compile-fixed/built/started → registry/tool set refreshes → subsequent agent turns can use the new service. - External distinction: the user asks for a capability absent from the current registered service/tool environment, or asks to extend/modify the existing system. - Future / prospective distinction: Go Micro models a desired future system/service capability relative to the current inventory rather than only selecting among existing tools; Design explicitly receives existing services and constructs the complete extended target design. - Adaptation option generated: one or more new/modified Go Micro services with schema/endpoints, generated handler/business logic and an updated managing agent/tool surface. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated services are compiled and started, allowed to register, micro chat refreshes discovered tools, and the new endpoints become immediately usable by the same operational agent; in initial generation mode the approved system becomes the subsequent running service/agent environment. - Boundary reachability: both micro chat autonomous missing-capability generation and micro run --prompt / CLI user-approved generation are shipped documented CLI modes. The positive mapping does not depend on repository CI or .github/loop dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode removing the model&#39;s missing-capability judgment/service-design choices leaves only a generator with no contextual adaptation decision. In parent mode the human decides whether the proposed adaptation is admitted, and that decision is returned through the same generator into executable capability. - Evidence: cmd/micro/chat/chat.go, cmd/micro/cli/generate/generate.go, cmd/micro/run/run.go, README.md, flow/analyze.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the adaptation path is developer/runtime oriented rather than production hot-patching of an arbitrary deployed service; the credited mode is nevertheless first-party and explicitly returns generated service capability into the supported running micro chat environment. flow.Analyze/LLMOptimizer alone would be only a partial adaptation constructor because applying its prompt proposal is caller-owned.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no product-runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included agent system prompts, auth/rules, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits, requestinput, generated-service confirmation and repository North Star/maintainer restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none of the product-runtime mechanisms escalates a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation at this recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S5 in the assessed product runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth rules/JWT wrappers, guardrails, tool approvals, static prompts/configuration, human input pauses, spend budgets and generation confirmation. - Closure path: ordinary task/action approvals and configuration changes can alter execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text, permission checks and human approvals bound ordinary operations; they do not by themselves own system identity or ultimate policy. Repository NORTHSTAR and off-limits maintainer decisions belong to the excluded repository-development organization. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, auth/, internal/docs/CONTINUOUSIMPROVEMENT.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a/assessments/go-micro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a\">9a9faa2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:40:17+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bhandiwad/agent-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pandita Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bhandiwad/agent-harness/tree/0827a48d5131537216e74cabd2acf78f0138686b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0827a48d5131537216e74cabd2acf78f0138686b\">0827a48 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed task execution and conversational/file/tool work with successive observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User changes, variable model output, local file contents, tool errors, context and permission limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses a tool name/arguments, delegates to an allowed specialist or returns a final answer; subsequent tool receipts can alter its next choice. - Decision owner: The configured assistant model, within operator-set tool schemas and permission bounds. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable session/turn history, PostgreSQL writer lock, budgets, installed adapters, native tools, separate approver and receipt validation. - Closure path: User turn → model generation → selected tool or final response → verified tool result/feedback appended → next agent model step or terminal answer. - Boundary reachability: The shipped Runner.tick and modelstep are wired from the installable worker and current config/agent.json gives the assistant selectable native file tools; a real provider endpoint must be configured by the operator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves authorization and state transitions, but no discretionary interpretation of the user task or choice among tools; hence S1 choice is agent-owned. - Evidence: installed config, model/tool loop, dispatch. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: High for first-party wiring, not for untested live model behavior. - Caveats: Endpoint/keys and tenant authorization need real deployment; API limits and approval are external constraints, not autonomous owner rights.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No shipped closed attenuation relation between distinct concurrently operating S1 organizations is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential conflicting specialist work or concurrent session writes; team handoff data and writer locking address different local hazards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No distinct inter-S1 coordination actor decides a response to concrete peer conflict; fixed team order and single-session concurrency prevent local overlap. - Decision owner: Root S1 chooses permitted delegation; deterministic session code serializes handoffs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Specialist team sequence, parent frames, Postgres writer lock and configured active-session capacity. - Closure path: Root task → specialist result → root context; no peer-interference observation → coordination judgment → revised independent S1 commitments loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequencing a hierarchy and blocking concurrent writes do not by themselves establish independent S1-unit oscillation regulation. - Evidence: team handoff/return, session ownership, storage. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A tenant could build a fleet with external coordination; no S2-specific packaged correction relation was established here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned whole-system current-control function over a portfolio of autonomous operations is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Session concurrency, budget exhaustion, cancellation, uncertain external writes and approval delays. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configuration/tenant owner sets limits and agent roster; runtime enforces caps and halts risky states, rather than autonomously revising whole-system resource/commitment priorities. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for configuration and human approver for action permission; model for its own S1 task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxsteps/tokens/time, session locks, access-control checks, approval digest and reconciliation guard. - Closure path: Fixed limit violation → fail/pause/deny/reconcile; no evidenced autonomous or legitimate parent current-control bargaining and returned cross-unit decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves static limit enforcement, not a discretionary portfolio-control decision. Team delegation is task decomposition, not S3 by name. - Evidence: config and capacities, run limits/recovery, approval/receipt enforcement. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A broader tenancy operation may have S3 outside this agent-service boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No default complementary, sufficiently independent audit of operational claims is wired into a corrective governance path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect answers, unconfirmed tool side effects and fabricated completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The optional completioncheck callback can reject format/content against a developer-supplied checker; tool receipts confirm transport outcomes, but no first-party separate reality-inspecting auditor makes an independent judgment. - Decision owner: Configured callback/adapter and deterministic runtime for validation; no S3 audit owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool result schema checks, write receipts, completion schema/check callbacks, test suite and event log. - Closure path: Model answer → optional direct callback → retry/fail, or tool invocation → receipt → model; this is routine completion/tool validation, not complementary audit access to independent operational reality. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An injected callback can be implemented by an external evaluator, but no shipped audit-specific independent actor/path is present or owned by a first-party standard mode. - Evidence: completion check and rejection, tool receipt validation. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: An application can install an independent checker, but generic callback extensibility alone does not supply a first-party S3 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No closed external-and-prospective adaptation cycle for future harness capability is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing model providers, tools, user needs and knowledge; context windows and new requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User/developer changes the model/tool configuration and prompts, while agent research occurs only within the current request. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for future capacity; same S1 model for present task response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Configured model aliases, skills, context history/archive, provider protocol adapter and optional specialist handoff. - Closure path: Manual config changes can affect later execution; no product-owned future-environment sensing → adaptation option judgment → feedback into present S3/capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context/history retention and task planning optimize current work; they do not authorize an agent to revise its organizational future capability. - Evidence: settings and agent contracts, context construction, installed config. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Separate maintenance-agent examples do not automatically wire future adaptation into the deployed generic assistant.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy dispute resolution loop at the assessed agent-service recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access violations, disallowed tools, untrusted input and action approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tenant operator and external identity authority supply authorizations and policy; independent actor approves a particular write action, not ultimate organizational identity. - Decision owner: External operator/IdP/approver for fixed permissions; none for a packaged S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RS256 identity verification, role permissions, immutable configuration digest, approval digest and untrusted-input isolation. - Closure path: Token/policy match → permitted/denied session/tool; action approval returns one tool gate, not an identity-level dispute → legitimate authority decision → enduring operating-policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent instructions explicitly treat tool output as data and cannot change policy authority. Human approval is narrow execution consent, not S5. - Evidence: authorization and config digest, approval logic, policy/identity config. - Basis: structural bounded absence. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A separate operating institution may exercise S5 outside the declared product boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ce4965715a8d34e1ca0c4292be0cfa66387ff968/assessments/pa/pandita-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ce4965715a8d34e1ca0c4292be0cfa66387ff968\">ce49657 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:45:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cUDGk/nipus-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nipus-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cUDGk/nipus-harness/tree/0c0349ee7f37d9bc0334c1d5ea7802c49f8e950b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c0349ee7f37d9bc0334c1d5ea7802c49f8e950b\">0c0349e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete bounded coding/research work and correct it against actual tool/acceptance feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source, failed checks, scope violations, incomplete answers and transient model/tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose inspections, edits, commands and repair strategy; declare finish or an evidenced blocker. - Decision owner: model worker instantiated by the first-party prompt/tool/continuation composition; optional declared child owns its bounded local work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pi session/model library, owned tools, boxing, request gates, acceptance runner, result/evidence/diff validation, persisted sessions and ledger. - Closure path: goal → tool-backed worker → finish → real checks/evidence/scope → reasons returned to same worker → revised work or honest terminal result. - Boundary reachability: CLI createSession wires the owned customTools and prompt, then directly invokes runLoop; dependency execution does not replace this composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the worker leaves checks/locks but no task-specific implementation or correction decisions. - Evidence: src/cli.ts; src/loop.ts; src/tools.ts; src/prompt.ts; src/session.ts; src/delegate.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Source reachability only; Windows/model prerequisites were not exercised. A mock provider is not credited as autonomous.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent destructive overlap among independent task-worker runs sharing folders and host jobs. - Distinct S1 units: separate CLI task/worker runs with their own goal, session and result on the same host. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writers in an identical or nested workspace undo each other&#39;s edits; overlapping Unity/heavy processes contend for an exclusive host resource; crashed owners may leave orphan processes. - Attenuating coordination relation: CLI lockWorkspace uses a short atomic table lock plus real-path exact/parent-child checks before acquiring a writer lock; hostJob waits on named owner-aware locks, detects a busy Unity project and reaps stale-owner processes before reuse. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: workspacebusy prevents a second writer from starting; busy host jobs queue until safe or return busy at timeout into worker/acceptance feedback. The lock is released after the owning task/process ends. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relations identify actual workspace/resource collision domains and modify whether/when another independent operational run may execute. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cross-run edit interference and exclusive host-process conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit, defer or reject conflicting runs under the fixed collision policy. - Decision owner: developer-authored lock/admission policy and caller-declared host lock identity; autonomous organizational coordination discretion is not composed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exclusive-create lock files, owner PID/start-time checks, workspace table, queue timeout, orphan cleanup and returned status. - Closure path: another active owner&#39;s collision → refusal/wait → subsequent run starts only after safe release or receives busy evidence. - Boundary reachability: src/cli.ts invokes lockWorkspace before work and releases it in finally; src/host.ts hostJob is reachable through declared tools and acceptance jobs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: specific coordination machinery is shipped, but its response is predetermined rather than chosen/revised by an autonomous coordinator. - Evidence: src/host.ts; src/cli.ts; src/tools.ts; tests/parallel.test.ts; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: C does not claim coordination across hosts/installations or semantic negotiation. Tests were inspected as witnesses, not executed here.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: whole-system inside-and-now control - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/gate.ts, src/commands.ts, src/cli.ts, src/delegate.ts - Closure path: Per-role/run quotas, account-limit enforcement, static tool/model profiles and host locks bound tasks. status --active exposes state and stop/resume provides local lifecycle intervention, but no shipped whole-system commitment/resource decision with a distinct supervisory owner and return contract is established. Generic caller control is not automatically parent S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/gate.ts; src/commands.ts; src/cli.ts; src/delegate.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: complementary independent audit - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/loop.ts, src/rules.ts, src/delegate.ts, tests/review.test.ts - Closure path: runLoop checks acceptance, evidence and diffs as the normal completion path and sends failures back to the same worker. Suspect violations and needsjudgment do not instantiate an independent source auditor. Arbitrary declared roles can be composed into review but no audit-specific independent claim/judgment/corrective loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/loop.ts; src/rules.ts; src/delegate.ts; tests/review.test.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: external and prospective capability adaptation - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/cli.ts, src/session.ts, src/loop.ts, src/prompt.ts - Closure path: Web retrieval serves the current research goal; session persistence, retry and resume retain ongoing work. No supported outside-and-then option-development/adoption path changes the worker&#39;s future repertoire; task/model configuration edits are inputs, not a closed parent adaptation function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/cli.ts; src/session.ts; src/loop.ts; src/prompt.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity and ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate variety and checked alternatives are recorded below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific path established. - Decision owner: ordinary operational agent, developer and caller rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/prompt.ts, src/cli.ts, src/gate.ts, src/loop.ts - Closure path: Fixed goals, task scope, forbidden terms, model/tool gates and clean-room declarations constrain operational execution. A blocked/needsjudgment result requests task-owner judgment, but no explicit ultimate identity/policy matter and authoritative return into standing governance is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: positive and supported parent alternatives were tested before this bounded absence conclusion. - Evidence: src/prompt.ts; src/cli.ts; src/gate.ts; src/loop.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded to the reviewed supported distribution; custom future extensions are not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83ba5c0624e2056ca6c034332624e473993f2f01/assessments/ni/nipus-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83ba5c0624e2056ca6c034332624e473993f2f01\">83ba5c0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T21:52:07+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mnbresearch/crewdeck\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CrewDeck</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mnbresearch/crewdeck/tree/7925ffb4c5e922f0081cce9ca59f62190b6e7864\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7925ffb4c5e922f0081cce9ca59f62190b6e7864\">7925ffb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured autonomous participant agents execute assigned coding/research/other tasks and return results into the company portfolio. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task requirements, tool outputs, errors and changing local work conditions faced by each participating agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: each invoked autonomous CLI/HTTP worker chooses how to perform its assigned task and returns an outcome; CrewDeck decides neither the model&#39;s work steps nor its answer. - Decision owner: the configured external agent actor for task-local S1 discretion; human/manual and simulation modes do not supply this autonomous state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: nextTaskFor, claim, context prompt assembly, shell/HTTP/remote adapters, run outputs, status transitions and persisted state. - Closure path: operator creates an assigned task → scheduler/remote claim supplies context to the AI agent → agent works and reports → completeRun records the outcome and result/cost → later tasks consume the result or the same work is retried/approved. - Boundary reachability: shell/HTTP agent adapters are shipped and directly called by the real server tick; remote participants have a shipped claim/complete protocol. The documented CLI examples require user-supplied providers and do not imply internal provider capabilities are CrewDeck&#39;s own metasystem. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the external agent makes meaningful task-specific execution decisions in the supported assembled operating mode; CrewDeck&#39;s deterministic queue/rules do not acquire that discretion. - Evidence: README launch and agent adapter modes, server dispatch and remote claim/complete, runner implementation. - Basis: structural, supported configuration and first-party wiring. - Confidence: medium-high for reachable assembled agent execution; no live proprietary provider run was performed in this review. - Caveats: neither browser sim nor a manual user task demonstrates agent ownership, and no foreign agent&#39;s S2–S5 is inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: explicit ownership/lock and dependency guards attenuate competing execution and premature consumption of work across distinct participating agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two agent workers claiming the same task/agent capacity concurrently and downstream work starting before its prerequisite result is available. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assigned task identity and dependency choice originate in portfolio configuration; deterministic first-party claim eligibility denies incompatible concurrent starts and releases locks on completion/timeout. - Decision owner: operator/configuration for assignment/dependency intent and deterministic runtime for fixed admission; there is no autonomous agent selecting/revising the inter-agent coordination policy at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task run lock with expiry, one-task-per-agent invariant, blockedBy, cycle checks and status/attempt state. - Closure path: candidate agent run is tested against assignment, live locks and completed prerequisites → conflicting claim is rejected or deferred → a completed/expired run releases the lock and changes subsequent eligibility. - Boundary reachability: the same claim and nextTaskFor invariants are called in real server dispatch and remote polling and are exercised by first-party engine tests. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the anti-contention path is function-specific and operational, but its decisive policy is configured/deterministic. An autonomous coordinating owner would require further composition; generic agent-to-agent conversation is not claimed. - Distinct S1 units: independently configured shell/HTTP/remote participants performing different assigned tasks at the company recursion. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing workers could otherwise double-execute a shared work item, or a consumer agent could act on incomplete producer output. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task/worker claims plus explicit completed-prerequisite gating and dependency-cycle rejection. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: denied claims remain unstarted; completion or timeout changes eligibility; dependent agents receive completed producer summaries only once prerequisite tasks reach done. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive witness is the source-enforced concurrent-claim conflict and consumption-of-incomplete-output prevention, not the mere existence of task ordering or a board. - Evidence: selection, claim, completion, lock expiry, claimed-node tests, real server dispatch. - Basis: structural first-party code and passing offline tests. - Confidence: medium; the lock conflict is direct, whereas broader semantic work-product interference is not resolved by this mechanism. - Caveats: no independent agent negotiates shared files or autonomously revises coordination policy.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the company operator reviews whole-portfolio current tasks, staff capacity and spend and sets effective resource, priority and commitment constraints. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing work, agent availability, constrained cost and tasks that fail or await review. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator creates/reassigns/prioritizes tasks, changes budgets/pauses and approves or requests rework across the company portfolio. - Decision owner: legitimate installed company owner/operator; the server merely enforces selected rules and makes current state visible. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /api/state and /api/summary, UI and editing API, budget accounting and hard dispatch stop, task review and status ledger. - Closure path: operator inspects shared current state → changes assigned worker, task priority or monthly budget, pauses an agent or rejects a reviewed task → saved state and next dispatcher tick govern subsequent commitments and execution. - Boundary reachability: the shipped self-hosted API exposes whole-company state and operator mutations that the same production dispatch path immediately consumes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an AI agent may perform an assigned task, but no shipped agent owns company-wide current prioritization or budget selection; numerical sorting and hard stops enforce human settings. - Whole-system current view: summary spans task statuses, active agents, total current spend and waiting reviews, with full state available to the board. - Current-control decision scope: organization-wide worker availability, priority, task assignment and cost envelopes, plus returned acceptance/rework control. - Evidence: summary and controls, budget/review gates, operating API. - Basis: explicit operator mode and structural return-to-dispatch. - Confidence: high for installed human current-control path. - Caveats: a human&#39;s acceptance of an individual deliverable alone is not S3; the positive mapping relies on the broader current portfolio view and cross-agent authority.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party complementary independent audit loop over operational truth is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false self-reported completion or faulty work would require evidence outside the ordinary participant result and routine operator approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not supplied as a distinct audit judgment; generic human approve/reject of the returned output is task acceptance. - Decision owner: no separate first-party auditor identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary result comments, run log, review state and operator approval. - Closure path: no complementary independently sourced findings-to-current-control path supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recordkeeping and task acceptance do not create independence from the agent&#39;s own report. - Evidence: completion and approval flow, adapter-reported result. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a separately configured reviewer agent could be given a task, but no shipped independent evidence path is wired as a company-level audit loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party externally/prospectively coupled capability-adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: market, user, model/tool or other future change is not sensed and turned into a runtime capability change by CrewDeck. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent; humans can edit the portfolio and agents can research as task work, but no shipped S4 feedback path selects future adaptations. - Decision owner: not established at the company boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: goals, routine task generation, company mission field and task comments. - Closure path: no separate outside/future model → adaptation proposal → current capability revision path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary planning and scheduled repetition do not establish S4. - Evidence: goal/task construction and routines, routine tick. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: high for inspected shipped composition. - Caveats: future adaptation could be implemented by external bots through generic APIs; such a hypothetical composition is not present evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy disagreement or exceptional matter is routed to legitimate final authority and returned as a governing resolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tension over company mission, ethos or final operating constraints is not handled by a separate runtime identity-policy loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5-level closed path; the human directly edits company name/mission and ordinary runtime settings. - Decision owner: human configuration authority exists, but no evidenced first-party S5 decision transaction is supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: company mission string in state/prompt, human API settings and approval/budget controls. - Closure path: static mission is passed into future task prompts; no surfaced identity matter → legitimate ultimate decision → returned resolution loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a mission string and task approvals are not an authoritative identity/policy decision process. - Evidence: company state and prompt, mission ingestion, company API and operator controls. - Basis: structural bounded negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the host human can always change values out-of-band, but this does not itself establish first-party S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85f36bfe535935b4130e12aef09c7644c3e53854/assessments/cr/crewdeck.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85f36bfe535935b4130e12aef09c7644c3e53854\">85f36bf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T18:28:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa1143/NamiDSH\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NamiDSH</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aa1143/NamiDSH/tree/629e9efcb121b2805a2f3c48565144b732c0ff7f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 629e9efcb121b2805a2f3c48565144b732c0ff7f\">629e9ef &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: A self-hosted model-driven session performs user-requested observation/tool actions and produces a revised answer to task/environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User tasks, changing available tools, local time/filesystem observations, returned tool values/errors and model-selected next steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In DeepSeek mode, the model determines assistant text or tool name/arguments from its session history and current advertised tool schemas; after receipts it can choose the next action or end. - Decision owner: Configured agent model through the shipped first-party agentLoop/DeepSeek adapter path; tool executor and host transport enforce choices without choosing the task strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxSteps=8 default bound, tool registration/list, JSON parsing and errors, pre-execute waterfall, event stream, abort signal, in-memory session log and restricted filesystem tool. - Closure path: User input → first-party webserver/session/loop → configured model tool decision → first-party ToolRuntime/local tool → logged result → next model turn/answer → web SSE response. - Boundary reachability: apps/web/src/main.ts actually installs spine, model adapter and webserver; the AgentLoop.run reached by HTTP executes the model/tool feedback loop with the shipped read-only FS/time tools. This is a supported opt-in real provider mode, not merely an external model callback skeleton. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses tool actions and final responses in configured DeepSeek mode. Deterministic mock fallback, model provider infrastructure and safety hooks do not themselves own that discretion. - Evidence: entry wiring, agent loop, DeepSeek SSE tool-call adapter, tools, session. - Basis: Explicit installed real-provider mode and first-party closed tool-result feedback; offline tests corroborate routing only. - Confidence: High for source reachability; no live paid DeepSeek E2E attempted. - Caveats: Mock mode is deterministic and all first-party file tools are read-only; neither implies broader system authority or external-provider correctness.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No coordination of multiple independently autonomous S1 work units with a specific attenuated interaction conflict is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plugin service name conflicts and simultaneous web sessions can occur, but these are registry/transport concerns rather than demonstrated inter-S1 organizational interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Cordis resolves service registration and dependency readiness; sessions remain independent records with no reciprocal task coordination decisions. - Decision owner: Deterministic registry/fiber scheduler and the operator who composes plugins. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Service uniqueness checks, plugin hot-unplug lifecycle, event channels and per-session SSE filters. - Closure path: Service availability changes reload dependent plugin implementations, not a feedback relation between two task-operating S1 cells. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A model sees its own session and tools, not conflicts between separate autonomous operational units. - Evidence: spine, tool registry, webserver, agent tests. - Basis: Bounded negative against shipped multi-session and plugin-interference candidates. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Future compositions could introduce many autonomous agents, but such a mode is not shipped.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-system current controller regulates a portfolio of autonomous S1 work, priorities and resource commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Individual session turn limits, client disconnects, tool errors and plugin availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model performs local S1 continuation; the host enforces fixed maxSteps and client cancellation. No second actor holds cross-session budget/resource/commitment discretion. - Decision owner: Local agent for its task choices; deterministic runtime and human operator for installed configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxSteps counter, AbortController, dependency readiness, session event log and tool pre-execute hooks. - Closure path: Limit/abort terminates or prevents one run; no whole-portfolio observation → S3 priority/resource decision → changed subsequent multi-unit operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stop enforcement and tool transport do not own whole-system current management merely because they can block an action. - Evidence: loop, webserver, tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A host could add an operator management plugin; generic plugin hooks do not constitute an installed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit pathway checks the actual agent&#39;s claims through alternate reality evidence and returns findings into control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool action mistakes and stale results can be tested or blocked, but product does not install a separate independent truth-checking auditor. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Optional pre-execute hooks can block action and unit tests verify mock behavior, neither produces an independent audit judgment from distinct operational evidence. - Decision owner: Operator-authored guard/test runner outside the packaged runtime; no S3 reviewer in standard deployed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool-result events, session logs, mocked agent tests and mock/real Cordis compatibility comparison. - Closure path: Direct tool result → same agent context; separate test/guard → local failure/block. No independent audit finding → accountable S3 corrective change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical testing and configurable permission hooks are not independent S3 just because a different function handles them. - Evidence: tool hooks, agent test guard, compatibility test. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Operator may externally audit runs; not a first-party packaged complementary audit channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective strategy/adaptation feedback loop is installed in the session&#39;s first-party distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tasks, plugin/provider availability, workspace changes and future model upgrades. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator may replace plugins/provider or edit persona; the agent plans only the present chat turn and follows current tool results. - Decision owner: Operator/developer for longer-term model/tool changes; no strategically deciding agent in the standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dynamic plugin dependency restart, configurable provider/model, ephemeral session log and prompt service. - Closure path: Current task → local reply, or manual plugin/config update → next run; no external forecast/adaptation option → S3 conversation → future capability change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A hot-swappable model service is an adaptation mechanism, not evidence that the agent models changing external capability and selects revisions. - Evidence: entry, spine, agent loop, plugin tests. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Educational articles explain extensibility, not installed strategic decision ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and returned mandate operates at the local harness recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Access to local file tools, persona changes, model credentials and user preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator preselects workspace, model and persona and may compose a permission guard; the shipped web agent lacks a channel for identity-level matters to a legitimate parent authority with returned policy. - Decision owner: Human developer/operator outside the agent session; static code and optional plugin veto enforce limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static system prompt, root-scoped tools, runtime configuration, dependency hooks and tool waterfall. - Closure path: Ordinary guard veto or user input influences one run; no identity/policy issue → ultimate authority → authoritative decision → subsequent harness governance feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Persona text, workspace permissions and generic approval-like hooks do not transfer the S5 decision right to the agent or establish a parent-governed closed mode. - Evidence: system prompt, entry, tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Maintainer governance of the public project is a separate organization, not the installed local session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/39aef6e78dd1ce2ff1a6b9a9b9fe4cba8b0f2ac2/assessments/na/namidsh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 39aef6e78dd1ce2ff1a6b9a9b9fe4cba8b0f2ac2\">39aef6e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:24:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ketiyohannes/mint\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mint</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ketiyohannes/mint/tree/0f98e1b4a17f9b73aabc6f8789e604d5902d648d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0f98e1b4a17f9b73aabc6f8789e604d5902d648d\">0f98e1b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce context-sensitive tutoring, exercises and judgments of demonstrated understanding. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different materials, questions, wrong/partial learner answers and requests for additional help. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose grounded explanation/practice content and judge an answer against materials; propose a curriculum or session wrap within the teaching contract. - Decision owner: model-backed tutor instantiated by the first-party job composition; learner owns goals and acceptance, code owns state transitions and safety limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SDK/app-server transport, folder/domain hooks, schemas and retry, topic-progress rules, JSON store, UI and exercise sandbox. - Closure path: learner answer → judgeCheck → validated verdict/feedback → submitCheck stores attempts and updates progress; next session/thread consumes refreshed context. tutorReply returns a grounded response into the continuing thread. - Boundary reachability: shipped ChatService/SessionService invoke the owned jobs through Kernel.runtime; the Claude/Codex adapters provide the configured execution seam. No development agent is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor, transport, allowlists and progress rules cannot choose the same explanations or misconception verdicts; Demo is explicitly scripted and is not the credited mode. - Evidence: src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts; src/main/services/threads.ts; src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for implemented ownership/wiring; no claim of learning efficacy. - Caveats: provider access and a real model are required; learner activity drives the sessions and accepted records.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for interference among independent S1 tutoring units at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is interference among independent S1 tutoring units; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/services/threads.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Closure path: Concurrent class planning, per-class locks and fresh-state merges prevent duplicate jobs or lost updates, but the reviewed cell exposes one tutor through several service facets. No specific inter-S1 mutual-adjustment relation is established by these storage primitives. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/services/threads.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for whole-system resource/commitment control at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is whole-system resource/commitment control; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/learning/engine.ts, src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Closure path: Curriculum selection, lesson progress and help-level changes are the primary tutoring transformation. Cancellation, fixed turn limits and a learner selecting a model do not constitute whole-system current resource bargaining or intervention. The inspected supported learner mode supplies no separate function-specific parent S3 loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/learning/engine.ts; src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/agents/claude.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for independent challenge to operational tutoring claims at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is independent challenge to operational tutoring claims; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/jobs/index.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts, src/main/jobs/run.ts. - Closure path: The model judges the learner&#39;s answer, not a tutor&#39;s ordinary claim of successful work. Diagram repair, schema validation and learner-code tests are routine production checks. No independent direct-evidence challenge of the tutoring cell with a returned corrective control path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts; src/main/jobs/run.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for external and prospective adaptation of the tutoring system&#39;s capability at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is external and prospective adaptation of the tutoring system&#39;s capability; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/jobs/index.ts, src/main/learning/engine.ts. - Closure path: Source import, web-assisted answers, curriculum re-planning and learner-independence history inform delivery of the current class. They do not develop and return options for changing this harness&#39;s future operational capability. No separate supported autonomous or parent adaptation loop is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/jobs/index.ts; src/main/learning/engine.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path for identity or ultimate-policy tension at the tutoring-cell boundary at the declared boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate distinction is identity or ultimate-policy tension at the tutoring-cell boundary; observed nearby mechanisms are assessed below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive right with the required closure established. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner identified for this function; learner/model/developer ownership of ordinary teaching remains as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/main/services/setup.ts, src/main/services/chat.ts, src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Closure path: Mission fields, fixed pedagogy, trusted domains and record/source acceptance constrain the teaching task. Changing a class goal or approving a source/record is ordinary operational input. No path frames an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy matter, routes it to a legitimate ultimate authority, and returns that decision to govern the cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness is supplied by the inspected alternatives, so agent/constructor/parent ownership is not inferred. - Evidence: src/main/services/setup.ts; src/main/services/chat.ts; src/main/services/sessions.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high, bounded to the supplied distribution. - Caveats: custom integrations or a wider institution could add this function; that possibility is not evidence of a shipped path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bff005c3dfa3f2e68b435916ea922264b1bacad1/assessments/mi/mint.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bff005c3dfa3f2e68b435916ea922264b1bacad1\">bff005c &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:09:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/evolution301/agent-control-tower\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agent-control-tower</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/evolution301/agent-control-tower/tree/14c6c98aa3ab741a5fe42afe10021d3b16deeb4d\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14c6c98aa3ab741a5fe42afe10021d3b16deeb4d\">14c6c98 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: configured role model performs a delegated task with context and workspace tools, reporting the resulting work product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous brief, changing code/file contents and command feedback encountered within a role assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the first-party OpenAI-compatible model loop selects tool calls and the next work step based on returned outputs, within a configured step budget. - Decision owner: role model in the supported OpenAI-compatible adapter; externally configured CLI/DSH agents also have task-local discretion, but their private internal autonomy is not imputed to the tower. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role prompt with mandate/write scopes, model-chat tool loop, shell execution, task assignment, artifacts and report intake. - Closure path: operator dispatches work → role model requests tool calls → host returns workspace observations → model adapts tool calls and returns a result → board records the report and the operator may accept or rework. - Boundary reachability: dispatch selects the shipped openai adapter and calls its own model-to-runshell loop; it is executable with a user-supplied compatible API endpoint and key, not merely an abstract pluggable interface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the substantive implementation steps; neither the CLI nor the operator predetermines each tool call. - Evidence: adapter model/tool loop; dispatch integration; README modes. - Basis: first-party control flow in a supported installed mode. - Confidence: high for reachable decision structure; no paid model execution claimed. - Caveats: mock-mode deterministic completion is not S1 evidence, and the shell tool is a host-enforced mechanism rather than an S1 decision owner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no materially closed, function-specific feedback relation regulating disturbances between distinct S1 units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potential collisions when roles edit the same workspace and receive overlapping instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role assignments and write-scope advice are configured by the operator; the CLI iterates a static batch and never detects or resolves competing edits across S1 agents. - Decision owner: no first-party S2 owner shown for inter-S1 disturbance feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-role workspace paths, instruction-level write scopes, optional batch spacing and task-key idempotency. - Closure path: dispatch routes tasks to roles and records status; no observed conflict-feedback → changed inter-role behavior relation is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routing, sequential iteration, distinct directories and prompts alone do not evidence autonomous/constructed S2 coordination. - Evidence: batch loop and serial gap; prompt write-scope text; role/task states. - Basis: bounded structural negative, not a statement that coordinated external agent teams are impossible. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: configured external DSH sessions may coordinate themselves, but their private inter-agent control is outside this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: a human lead supervises the entire local work portfolio and intervenes in role commitments, acceptance and rework. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished tasks, failures, evidence gaps and attention overload across active roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator chooses role and brief, changes the role roster, sees all task statuses and attention, accepts or sends work back for another attempt. - Decision owner: local lead/operator in the documented self-hosted mode; not the model running a delegated S1 task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: full board/snapshot, attention list, inbox, audit ledger, role-add/remove and accept/rework commands. - Closure path: operator inspects whole-board/attention state → assigns new work or selects rework, acceptance and role setup → mutated task/role state governs next dispatch and future capacity allocation. - Boundary reachability: the same CLI exposes both a complete task-role view and actionable role/dispatch/rework commands against the same persisted board used by actual adapters. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead owns cross-role current commitments; transitions, status views and retry counters are deterministic supporting mechanics, not autonomous resource decisions. - Whole-system current view: buildsnapshot/tower board aggregates role states, tasks in flight and attention across the workspace, not just an individual report. - Current-control decision scope: selection/assignment of current work to roles, who remains active on the roster, acceptance of commitments and intervention on rework/escalation. - Evidence: board and attention commands; dispatch/accept/rework; role roster commands; board task state. - Basis: complete first-party operator control loop. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human acceptance alone would not be S3; the claim also requires the whole-role view and operational role/assignment changes. The dashboard is read-only; decisions return through CLI.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a separately invoked verification path checks report claims against actual workspace artifacts and configured executable commands before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false positive claims that files exist or checks passed when no underlying artifact/output supports them. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured verifier requirements select mandatory report evidence, filesystem checks and optional real commands; the operator invokes acceptance and determines subsequent rework. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic verification constructor plus explicit human acceptance owner; no independently deciding AI auditor is bundled. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifyreport, file existence checks, subprocess command execution, structured findings and mandatory rejection-to-rework path. - Closure path: agent submits ordinary markdown report → accept invokes verifier&#39;s workspace filesystem and real commands → failed independent observations produce problems → CLI transitions to rework; passing checks allow accepted → affected task&#39;s next execution/acceptance changes. - Boundary reachability: verification is called directly in shipped cmdaccept and cmddispatch; optional verifycommands are supported configuration, with non-optional file-existence checks always executed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: complementary observations are independent of the report text but the checks are deterministic/configured and the ultimate decision is human; hence constructor-mode C, not autonomous A. - Claim being audited: a role&#39;s report that an artifact exists and specified checks were performed and succeeded. - Ordinary reporting path: parsereport ingests model-authored sections (Files, Checks, Summary, Status) into task/report and inbox. - Complementary access path: verifier reads actual workspace paths and executes configured shell verification commands with return codes and recorded output tails. - Independence boundary: filesystem and command exit codes do not rely on the model&#39;s natural-language claim; however same workspace is writable by the agent and config may omit executable checks, so this is limited evidence of artifact existence/command results, not independent work-quality certification. - Who acts on findings: cmdaccept refuses transition to accepted and puts the task into rework; operator can re-dispatch under configured round limit. - Evidence: verification implementation; accept and rework control; reported-content parsing. - Basis: real alternate evidence path and mandatory gate; constructor classification. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a malicious role could manipulate shared files, and the verifier does not independently understand semantic correctness; stronger audit requires configured commands or an independent reviewer with separate trust boundary.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped loop senses external/future changes and revises the tower&#39;s capability or operating model as an adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future environmental change is not an independent monitored input into tower adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: missing; an operator can modify role configuration and agents can be asked to research, but no built-in future/environment intelligence owner returns adopted changes. - Decision owner: no S4 actor in the assessed tower. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: manually edited roles/config, briefs, history and optional external model work. - Closure path: no first-party external/prospective sensing → adaptive option → decided capability change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated suggestions within a task and generic ability to edit configuration do not establish a returned future-directed adaptation function. - Evidence: configuration; role setup and manual CLI changes; architecture. - Basis: bounded source negative. - Confidence: high for inspected first-party behavior. - Caveats: a separately created strategy role is theoretically possible but not instantiated as S4 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no function-specific ultimate-policy/identity conflict resolution loop with legitimate authority and return is furnished. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing ultimate mandates or exceptional organizational policy issues are not represented as a distinct operating transaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator supplies briefs and role policies in configuration, but no S5 question-to-final-authority-and-back path is established. - Decision owner: operator controls the config outside any demonstrated S5 procedure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static role descriptions, mandate metadata (requester, strength, policy) and user-issued task commands. - Closure path: ordinary task mandates are persisted and read during execution; no ultimate-policy issue → adjudication → authoritative resolution → governed future operation loop is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: passing an operator policy string to a role and enforcing task-specific rounds is not deciding collective identity. - Evidence: mandate creation; board mandate storage; role configuration. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: high for installed tower. - Caveats: the human operator has ultimate out-of-band control of a local installation, but authority alone does not prove a first-party S5 feedback loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b79a4a0daf889bd8c1990c95d0a989217dbd2e12/assessments/ag/agent-control-tower.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b79a4a0daf889bd8c1990c95d0a989217dbd2e12\">b79a4a0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T12:34:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Locotir/nexo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">nexo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Locotir/nexo/tree/a9643683250aeff6050d0b77adfed0fe9392e53c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a9643683250aeff6050d0b77adfed0fe9392e53c\">a964368 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute coding requests by repeatedly choosing tools, inspecting source/environment feedback, revising edits and returning an answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar repository structure, failed commands/tests, permission denials, context-window pressure and changing task instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next coding/tool action or task subagent after inspecting the prior feedback and judge when the task response is complete. - Decision owner: Model-driven agent in the shipped runturn loop; the human supplies the task and approval boundary, while Rust handles transport and enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, permission gate, max turns/cost, cancellation, context curator, sessions/checkpoints, loop/stale guards. - Closure path: User goal → model decision → first-party tool dispatch/permission → result or refusal appended to history → next model decision/final response. - Boundary reachability: turn::run constructs session, client, tools and optional SubAgent; runturn is directly used by the supported CLI/headless composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The substantive tool and code choices are returned by the model; the fixed gates do not select the edits. - Evidence: turn.rs#L165-L240, turn.rs#L1221-L1440, turn.rs#L1830-L1980. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the shipped decision/feedback loop. - Caveats: No paid-provider execution or benchmark success was independently reproduced in this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party cross-S1 conflict-and-oscillation regulation is established beyond bounded delegation and tool safety. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent edits could interfere, but the shipped task design runs file-changing children in foreground and confines background children to read-only work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Detect an inter-S1 collision and choose a mutual adjustment returned into two operational cells; no such S2-specific decision path is shipped. - Decision owner: No qualifying S2 actor; deterministic subagent mode restrictions and the parent task selection manage task execution locally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only profile, foreground writing, task report, shared host permission gate and session job tracking. - Closure path: Child task → report → parent coding loop; no evidenced two-unit collision signal and negotiated/adjusted subsequent worker behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent delegation and a serial-write safety rule are not the four-part S2 disturbance/attenuation/feedback witness. - Evidence: subagent.rs#L1-L96, subagent.rs#L174-L233, turn.rs#L165-L190. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high within the standard distribution. - Caveats: A user can construct a multi-session development team externally; that is not established by nexo&#39;s built-in task tool.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately established whole-system current control over a portfolio of operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overspend, repetitive tool loops and stuck goal continuation inside one session. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reallocate shared priorities/resources across distinct S1 units after a whole-current judgment; not supplied. - Decision owner: User chooses budget/permission limits, model chooses ordinary local work, deterministic runtime enforces stops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session budget, max-turns, day cost, repeat/stale guards, job kill/status and permission modes. - Closure path: Guard stops or constrains the same coding turn; no whole-system allocation decision returns to multiple operational units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A cap or kill mechanism is enforcement, not an agent-owned S3 resource bargaining decision. - Evidence: turn.rs#L1221-L1277, turn.rs#L455-L527. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The human user&#39;s wider coding organization was outside this session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently independent complementary audit of production claims with returned corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False coding completion claims or incorrect edits could require evidence outside the ordinary model/tool report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample actual code/task reality, decide a challenge finding and cause control response; no built-in audit-specific right is supplied. - Decision owner: None established; model-selected explore children provide generic task evidence, user may inspect results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only subagent, ordinary tests, session/event history, diagnostics and CLI view. - Closure path: Child search/report returns to the same S1 model; not a separate audit judgment whose finding drives accountable correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent context or routine command/test does not by itself make a complementary S3 control channel. - Evidence: subagent.rs#L1-L96, turn.rs#L600-L620. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A human could compose a separate reviewer workflow, but generic capability is not a shipped S3 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separate prospective environmental sensing and durable adaptation-option loop into future coding capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model context pressure, stale file reads and changed task information are regulated as present coding context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Assess external/future changes and select a persistent change to harness capability, then return that option into present operations; not found. - Decision owner: Context selection is deterministic; agent makes current coding choices; model/provider configuration changes are user-owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Curator, reusable brief, goal continuation, model/provider configuration and session memory/recall. - Closure path: Selected history/notes enter the next current request, not an enduring outside-and-then adaptation proposal and current-capability revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context engineering and task planning do not establish prospective S4 capability governance. - Evidence: brief.rs#L1-L26, turn.rs#L685-L737, context.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A model may write future-facing plans as ordinary user-assigned work; they are not automatically harness-level S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-policy dispute and authoritative identity-decision return loop at the selected coding session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tools, untrusted repository agent files and permission escalation during local coding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide an identity/ultimate-policy matter and return a binding revised policy to future operation; not supplied as a function-specific first-party path. - Decision owner: User/developer sets policy and explicitly approves individual actions; runtime enforces it without deciding the harness&#39;s identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan mode, trusted project filters, tool permission gate, prompt directives and persisted allow rules. - Closure path: User authorizes/refuses an individual tool and next tool action follows that result; no distinct identity-policy appeal → legitimate parent judgment → new binding organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission policy and normal task approvals are not S5 governance by their existence. - Evidence: permission.rs#L1-L86, turn.rs#L165-L190, subagent.rs#L75-L118. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer governance of the Rust product is a different recursion; it cannot be imported as runtime S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bf3ab640c4094d36677b56db543f71f472f16f16/assessments/ne/nexo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bf3ab640c4094d36677b56db543f71f472f16f16\">bf3ab64 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:29:55+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Eric2i/minimal-terminal-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MTA (Minimal Terminal Agent)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Eric2i/minimal-terminal-agent/tree/14147f6bbad6365a78b8112889958d109276c38b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14147f6bbad6365a78b8112889958d109276c38b\">14147f6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a user terminal task through model-selected shell commands and subsequent observation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown container state, command errors, dependency installation, timeout/exit codes and evolving task files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses bash arguments, next calls and submit completion after seeing tool results. - Decision owner: Configured inference model acting through first-party Agent.step; runtime executes only selected tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OpenAI-compatible adapter, Docker isolated environment, BashTool return code/output and configured step/command-time limits. - Closure path: User CLI task→model generation→selected bash→actual Docker result→tool message→next model choice or submit. - Boundary reachability: mta.cli.run constructs and executes the full route against a configured model endpoint, not merely a benchmark-only stub. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dynamic task action is chosen by model from observed real outputs; tool/runtime merely execute and cap work. - Evidence: task loop, CLI, shell tool. - Basis: Executable first-party model/tool/observation cycle with distinct task/termination decisions. - Confidence: High for first-party source reachability; external model quality untested. - Caveats: The model endpoint and Docker runtime must exist; this review did not execute paid API/Docker E2E.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No balancing of disturbances between separate autonomous S1 operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple commands may conflict inside one container, but they are decisions of the same serial S1 episode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The single model selects sequential commands; no inter-agent priority/resource arbitration actor. - Decision owner: Single S1 model and static Docker lifecycle settings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ordered tool calls, container boundary and timeout. - Closure path: A tool&#39;s result returns to the same model; no two distinct S1 actors→coordination decision→revised commitments. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A list of tools, trajectories or benchmark parallel runs is not a coordinating system. - Evidence: agent, environment. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External infrastructure could schedule many independent MTA jobs, outside this distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separately owned present-time organizational oversight of multiple autonomous operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task overruns, failures and token cost are bounded only at a single episode level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fixed step limits and timeout/exception handlers terminate or record status; no model-driven resource negotiation or portfolio reallocation. - Decision owner: CLI operator sets task and caps; program implements deterministic guardrails. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentConfig.steplimit, timeout, exitstatus, cancellation and Docker disposal. - Closure path: Over-limit or exception→terminal trajectory status, not S3 whole-system control/reassignment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A static maximum and error record constrain S1 but do not create a discretionary S3 manager. - Evidence: run control, CLI configuration. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Users may manage multiple runs manually; such activity is not packaged first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently owned runtime challenge of a worker&#39;s reported task completion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Inaccurate submit, misleading command output and task-result gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: submit ends an episode on model request; recorder logs that event, not a contrary inspection/judgment. - Decision owner: Same S1 model for any verification command; external evaluator/operator for later correctness checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Full trajectory, tool exit metadata, replay fields, benchmark Harbor adapters and final status. - Closure path: Worker self-check→submit→record; no separate evidence acquisition→independent audit verdict→corrective S3 control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Capturing trace/logprobs is not an independent inspector with a return route; terminal-benchmark grading is outside normal first-party operation. - Evidence: submit/tool schema, recording, agent. - Basis: Bounded absence review of trace/replay and submit paths. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external evaluator could grade stored episodes afterward; absent installed corrective return.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective environment intelligence and capability adaptation control over future agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model endpoint changes, tool capabilities, future tasks and reproducibility settings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human/configuration provides model endpoint, image and optional tools; task model solves current request. - Decision owner: Operator/researcher, not a separate strategic actor in MTA. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider-agnostic config, Docker image selection and recorded token data for offline research. - Closure path: Operator config or offline research may improve a future release; no shipped future scanning→option decision→governed operational adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Providing replay/training data is an input to outside research, not an in-runtime foresight decision system. - Evidence: config, CLI, trajectory. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external researcher may train a better model; not a first-party live S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate organizational identity/policy decision cycle. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shell risk, network access, task instructions and user trust. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI operator sets Docker network/image/limits and prompt, not an autonomous policy body. - Decision owner: External operator/developer; static system prompt and Docker arguments encode constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Docker isolation, optional --network none, max steps and standard system prompt. - Closure path: User configuration→bounded task execution; no identity dilemma→legitimate mandate choice→enforced policy return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A prompt telling the model to act safely and a container boundary are not ultimate self-governance. - Evidence: system prompt, Docker config, CLI. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: External institutional policies can constrain deployment; not part of the reviewed first-party harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5fe7ecd7a485a6fccaf7499252b87fa12ef26a6c/assessments/mt/mta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5fe7ecd7a485a6fccaf7499252b87fa12ef26a6c\">5fe7ecd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"3\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T22:29:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sergeiwallace/ai-chassis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ai-chassis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>3</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sergeiwallace/ai-chassis/tree/ea39de12db1308c2a46ac7ffab38ead14ce39aaf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ea39de12db1308c2a46ac7ffab38ead14ce39aaf\">ea39de1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: externally installed Claude Code/Codex coding-agent sessions work on assigned repository tasks using first-party shared context, skills, tools and linked worktrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code changes, errors and command/test observations within individual software tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a configured AI coding agent selects implementation, tool invocations and revisions after inspecting source and feedback; scripts constrain provenance and filesystem/Git operations. - Decision owner: active Claude/Codex model-backed coding session in the documented assembled mode; external provider internals not automatically credited above task level. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-machine context composer, installed skills (spec, commit, quality-check, shadow-codex-launch), Git/worktree helpers and provenance commits. - Closure path: human/controller assigns a repository task → installed coding agent reads the composed context and uses tools in its linked worktree → model adapts to observed source/tests → writes/commits work with provenance → review/merge or rework returns into later agent work. - Boundary reachability: the project ships installation and context-application CLI and actual SKILL.md entry points for Claude/Codex tools, explicitly assembling these available external agents; no proprietary private engine is required to run the published subset. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the live model chooses code and tool steps, unlike the deterministic provenance, commit and job-ledger checks. - Evidence: README usage and supported sessions; public CLI composition; planning skill; validated commit skill. - Basis: supported first-party agent integration and task feedback. - Confidence: medium-high for assembled decision structure. - Caveats: ai-chassis is not a model server; the actual provider and its private decision logic remain external, and no paid-provider E2E was run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: worktree admission, idempotent job tracking and unique result claims prevent some inter-session collisions and duplicate work or handoff effects. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two coding sessions operating in the same Git worktree, duplicate job submissions, competing terminal result claimants and repeated cross-host message sends. - Decisive decision or feedback right: worktree/owner/transport choices are configured or selected by an operator/agent; the first-party supervisor rejects invalid main-worktree launch and conflicting result claims and idempotently returns the existing job ID. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party coordination constructor, not a model that negotiates or revises conflict policy across S1 units. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: requirelinkedworktree, Ledger.createidempotent, locked claim, job status/ack, worktree naming and one-transport message-board route. - Closure path: incoming work is checked against linked worktree and request key → invalid/duplicate work is refused or bound to existing job; separate terminal claimants cannot both own the fresh result → sender resolves one handoff transport and receives corresponding status → later S1 work uses the non-duplicated job/message outcome. - Boundary reachability: the Python jobsupervisor has a runnable daemon/submit/status/claim/ack CLI, and the installed message-board SKILL names concrete transport and delivery procedures for working sessions; these are not mere roadmap diagrams. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a function-specific interference attenuation path exists, but the inter-S1 decision policy is configured and enforced by code. Additional autonomous coordination and guaranteed semantic non-conflict are not established. - Distinct S1 units: independently running coding agents bound to separate linked worktrees, and possible peer sessions on another host. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate job starts/result claims and shared working-tree edits would corrupt attribution or overwrite another agent&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: separate linked worktree admission, idempotency keys and exclusive claim with freshness checks; the transport ladder prevents duplicate handoff across channels. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: duplicate submit returns the original job instead of starting another; rejected claim refuses a second consumer; incompatible source tree cannot launch; a completed handoff gives a single actionable receipt. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source-level conflict checks forbid simultaneous incompatible owner claims and duplicated launches, not merely pass text or schedule tasks. - Evidence: job supervisor worktree and lock checks; idempotent submit and claim; exclusive result claim; message-board transport contract. - Basis: released runtime mechanisms with first-party ownership boundaries. - Confidence: medium-high for narrow duplication/claim interference. - Caveats: no generalized agent-authored negotiation or automatic resolution of competing code edits is supplied; worktrees isolate rather than semantically merge changes.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: the published cross-repository review/integration constructor inventories present fleet Git work, determines admissible ship/merge paths under explicit configured policy and returns readback status. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple pending branches and worktrees, missing or stale integration evidence, unauthorized merge requests and uncertain work ownership across repositories. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an authorized operator/repository policy must specify eligible integration paths; source code computes applicable candidates and refutes or executes eligible current integration. No first-party autonomous portfolio owner is shipped. - Decision owner: current operator/configured repository merge policy, supported by deterministic first-party multi-repo decision-and-feedback constructor; code does not acquire independent organizational authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: gitreview multi-repository discovery/inventory, shipmerge preflight, executemerge, exact terminal readback, job status and status-update skill. - Closure path: review runs over registered repositories and active worktrees → inventories candidate work and current status → configured opt-in proof allows/refuses/queues integration → any authorized action is executed → authoritative merge readback updates current review/reclamation behavior. - Boundary reachability: scripts/gitreview.py is published alongside the CLI-installed checkout and the shipped /git-review procedure explicitly invokes it to inspect and act; no private Chief/Firstmate live runtime is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party path is specific to fleet-level current integration, not merely a task-local merge button, but its decision rights are configured and the actual adapting agent/parent supervisor is not established as an autonomous owner at this fleet boundary; hence constructor C only. - Whole-system current view: gitreview.run discovers repositories from its configured projects root/registry and reports branches, worktrees, stashes, candidate merges and failures with a fleet-wide workflow state. - Current-control decision scope: which current branch/worktree work may be shipped or merged, which evidence/owners block integration and whether a follow-up reclamation is safe; no claim over broad business budgets or strategy. - Evidence: full multi-repo review procedure; Git reviewer orchestration and readback; status-update current-view procedure. - Basis: first-party specific review/ship-merge constructor. - Confidence: medium; only narrow Git integration current-control is established. - Caveats: no independent continuous AI chief is included in this claim; human consent on one code edit or PR alone would not justify P for the whole fleet, and deterministic merge proofs are support rather than autonomous decision rights.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a first-party review/audit packet and provenance-receipt constructor supplies separately read Git/test evidence for a reviewer, with typed audit findings and bounded closure checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: accepting an author&#39;s unverified claim that an implementation meets scope and has passed checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an explicitly invoked reviewer may inspect actual Git diff/test artifacts and return findings; first-party scripts require well-formed evidence and record disputed/open versus closed findings. No independent auditor model is bundled as an authority. - Decision owner: first-party audit-path constructor and user-supplied distinct reviewer; no automatic independent model verdict or fully autonomous S3 owner proven. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewpacket.pushdiff reads exact ratcheted Git range, supplies acceptance criteria/analyzer/test evidence; auditreceipt holds typed audit/fix/halt/terminal event records and computes closure state; quality-check requests external review with read-only Git fallback. - Closure path: author claims completed work → first-party code builds review packet from actual Git diff, test result and ratchet findings rather than author prose → separately designated reviewer can return findings → typed event ledger marks issues open, fixed or needing another review; integration proceeds only through separately authorized Git policy/readback. This is an optional assembled check, not a guaranteed global merge gate. - Boundary reachability: scripts/reviewpacket.py is a runnable CLI that opens Git itself; scripts/auditreceipt.py has executable state/render commands; published /quality-check invokes review/fallback, allowing construction of the path without undocumented private helpers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent evidence is available to a distinct reviewer, but reviewer selection, critical judgment and enforcement into all merges require further composition. The first-party function-specific constructor is present (C), not an autonomous first-party A auditor. - Claim being audited: a coding agent&#39;s proposed change satisfies acceptance criteria and reported checks. - Ordinary reporting path: agent-produced completion summary and proposed commit/PR. - Complementary access path: raw repository Git diff of attested range plus separate analyzer findings and test result, structurally validated against typed audit events. - Independence boundary: raw Git/test access does not depend solely on author&#39;s narrative; however the nominated model reviewer, external hlg executable, analyzer and test runner may be separate/outside this public distribution. - Who acts on findings: a reviewing agent/operator processes issues and chooses rework or policy-authorized merge; the constructor records open-blocking, fixed, deferred and terminal outcomes for follow-up. - Evidence: review packet raw Git range and test data; audit receipt typed state; quality-check caveat. - Basis: source-first constructed independent observation path. - Confidence: medium-low for real deployed independent reviewer closure; high that raw Git/test packet code exists. - Caveats: hlg review is an external requirement, not included in the credited surfaces; no claim of mandatory, independent reviewer execution or soundness of all audit verdicts.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped fleet-level environmental/future intelligence loop is shown that selects and integrates an adaptation to current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider, project and market changes can be studied by individual agents, but there is no first-party S4 adaptation authority at fleet recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: missing; the human can edit context and install skills, while persist can record user-supplied conventions. - Decision owner: no first-party separate S4 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: extensible skills/config, persist, roadmap and repo-level planning templates. - Closure path: no future-environment analysis → adaptation choice → changed operating capability loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retaining working conventions and writing a spec for a requested task are current work or operator customization, not S4 environmental adaptation. - Evidence: published roadmap and feature limits; persist skill; spec skill. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human can compose a capable future-monitoring agent using the tools, but the composition is not demonstrated at this public revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-level governance loop resolving ultimate-purpose or conflicting final policy matters is first-party operationally closed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing ultimate commitments or exceptional mission disagreements do not become a routed S5 matter in the installed agent fleet. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human-configured install profiles, Git merge opt-in and dangerous-action checks set constraints, but do not enact identity-policy issue adjudication. - Decision owner: legitimate human and host repository authorities for out-of-band policy; no separate first-party S5 decision chain credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: context rules, approvals, protected secrets/provenance gates and merge policy config. - Closure path: policy settings constrain present actions; no policy conflict is elevated to a legitimate final authority, resolved and fed back as a new collective decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a safety default or sign-off gate has no deliberated identity question and no ultimate organizational authority loop. - Evidence: shared rules; explicit spec approval; Git integration policy. - Basis: bounded structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: permissioned human policy control exists outside the audit&#39;s claimed S5 operational loop; an external CI branch-protection mechanism is not acquired as a first-party S5 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/43bcd6890a66d7cdb729c6bccfc1c2a67b13af84/assessments/ai/ai-chassis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 43bcd6890a66d7cdb729c6bccfc1c2a67b13af84\">43bcd68 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T20:41:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/purplemacaron/blank\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Blank</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/purplemacaron/blank/tree/e7a48c0445599217f8fa2b7320dee6b446735345\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e7a48c0445599217f8fa2b7320dee6b446735345\">e7a48c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform concrete workspace/research/code/document tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown files, requested changes, tool errors and provider failure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select tools/edits and revise work from returned results until a response or bounded stop. - Decision owner: main model actor and configured subagents within first-party tool loops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider streaming, allowlists/approval policy, tool dispatch, session persistence, maximum steps, compaction and fallback. - Closure path: assistant tool calls → executeTool → saved tool messages → subsequent model turn; reports/results return to user and main agent. - Boundary reachability: desktop IPC and bin/blank.js instantiate the shipped runAgent; child execution is implemented in runSubagent with its own history and tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model actor the dispatcher and store cannot choose task-specific operations; provider transports do not own those choices. - Evidence: src/core/agent.js; src/core/tools.js; src/core/prompt.js; electron/ipc.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Local/provider permissions bound action; no paid-model or desktop E2E run claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: inter-S1 conflict attenuation - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate organizational variety is considered in the checked alternatives below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: ordinary agent/user/developer rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/core/agent.js, src/core/worktree.js, src/core/tools.js - Closure path: delegatemany/teamdelegate fan out tasks in the same workspace and aggregate reports; their limits are fan-out bounds. Exact file edits and manually created worktrees are not tied here to a shipped worker collision-settlement loop. No explicit inter-worker interference feedback protocol is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness remains after the supported alternatives are distinguished. - Evidence: src/core/agent.js; src/core/worktree.js; src/core/tools.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in the reviewed distribution, not impossibility of a custom extension.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: whole-system current resource/commitment control - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate organizational variety is considered in the checked alternatives below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: ordinary agent/user/developer rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/core/agent.js, src/core/schedules.js, electron/ipc.js, src/core/approvals.js - Closure path: Main-agent planning/delegation is local task execution. Static per-run step limits, at most two scheduled runs, cancellation and session UI do not provide a separate whole-system resource bargaining/intervention decision. Generic local/remote approvals do not close a function-specific parent S3 mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness remains after the supported alternatives are distinguished. - Evidence: src/core/agent.js; src/core/schedules.js; electron/ipc.js; src/core/approvals.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in the reviewed distribution, not impossibility of a custom extension.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: a specifically configured complementary source-risk review of operational work. - Claim being audited: an implementation or proposed result is correct, secure and adequately tested. - Ordinary reporting path: the main/worker&#39;s answer and its own reported checks. - Complementary access path: separate read-only Critic run can inspect workspace source and gitdiff rather than merely repeat the producer transcript; bundled Code review procedure names diff/call-site/evidence checks. - Independence boundary: new child prompt/history, default Critic write=false and direct read/grep/glob/projectmap/gitdiff tools. The generic main-agent self-review is not credited. - Who acts on findings: the caller/main actor receives reports and remains responsible for verification; a claim-specific corrective and recheck contract must still be composed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer blind spots and unverified correctness/security/test claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge concrete source risks and return evidence-backed findings. - Decision owner: configured Critic agent for judgment; the caller/developer still supplies claim selection and corrective closure authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: team configuration, read-only child tools, report aggregation and optional role model. - Closure path: review-specific brief → isolated direct-evidence Critic → report tool result to main; no shipped mandatory fix/recheck or automatic audit acceptance transaction is established. - Boundary reachability: team config ships an audit-specific Critic role and teamdelegate instantiates it in the ordinary product; the bundled Code review skill supplies source-oriented review procedure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a function-specific audit construction path is present, but fully autonomous audit/control closure is not established merely by calling a generic specialist. - Evidence: src/core/config.js; src/core/agent.js; src/core/prompt.js; assets/skills/code-review/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; constructor only, no claimed ready-made audit loop. - Caveats: Team is off by default. A role named Critic alone would be insufficient; the C claim is limited to the explicit separate-context source-risk inspection path and leaves corrective composition unresolved.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt future assistance capability to evidenced recurring user needs and corrections. - External distinction: user preferences, repeated corrections and recurring workflows appear in recent sessions, contrasted with current durable memory and skills. - Future / prospective distinction: reflection explicitly requires durable facts useful weeks later and skills for workflows actually performed that will recur; transient task details are excluded. - Adaptation option generated: model proposes memory, a standing instruction or a reusable SKILL.md, with reason/confidence, rather than silently changing the system. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator Apply calls learn:apply → applyInsight updates memory/customInstructions or creates an actual skill; subsequent runAgent reloads skills/memory and buildSystemPrompt consumes instructions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated user-specific demand and correction that the existing capability repertoire fails to retain. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which proposed durable capability/instruction changes are adopted. - Decision owner: local operator using the learning proposal Apply action; model owns proposal content, not final adoption. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reflection model, validated insight store, IPC/UI action, memory/config/skill writers and next-turn prompt construction. - Closure path: external user evidence → future-oriented reusable proposal → owner applies selected insight → present repertoire/instructions change → later sessions use it. - Boundary reachability: manual learn:reflect and optional maybeReflect are wired in Electron IPC; learn:apply is the shipped adoption handler, not a development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: automatic reflection generates proposals only. The final adaptation judgment remains operator-owned; passive history or arbitrary memory addition is not the credited witness. - Evidence: src/core/learn.js; electron/ipc.js; src/core/agent.js; src/core/prompt.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the explicit future-reuse/adoption path. - Caveats: Not every insight is S4; the witness is externally evidenced recurring demand developed into future capability. No autonomous A base or empirical improvement claim.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: identity or ultimate-policy closure - Disturbance / variety regulated: the candidate organizational variety is considered in the checked alternatives below. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-specific decisive path established. - Decision owner: ordinary agent/user/developer rights do not establish this distinct function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: src/core/prompt.js, src/core/approvals.js, src/core/config.js, src/core/learn.js - Closure path: System identity, project instruction files, learned preferences, modes and permission approval matrices bound execution. The user can accept an insight or grant a tool action, but no first-party path identifies and resolves a constitutional/ultimate-policy issue with a returned authoritative settlement. Adaptation of a workflow or preference is not automatically S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no positive functional witness remains after the supported alternatives are distinguished. - Evidence: src/core/prompt.js; src/core/approvals.js; src/core/config.js; src/core/learn.js. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for source wiring; not an empirical outcome claim. - Caveats: Bounded absence in the reviewed distribution, not impossibility of a custom extension.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/795ffc52ee89b9f6039c62aaac16c775f1931dc9/assessments/bl/blank.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 795ffc52ee89b9f6039c62aaac16c775f1931dc9\">795ffc5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:41:53+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Ember452/baldric\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Baldric</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Ember452/baldric/tree/fa92656923649291a52de1e05d77d5c72a4976e6\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fa92656923649291a52de1e05d77d5c72a4976e6\">fa92656 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Implement user coding requests by iteratively choosing tools, inspecting outputs, revising edits and deciding when to answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown file layouts, failing commands, invalid tool arguments, changed requirements, provider/tool interruption and context limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model selects tool name/arguments and next coding action based on prior source and tool results. - Decision owner: First-party model-directed runagentloop in a supported AgentSession; user provides the goal and project trust. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in read/write/edit/bash tools, provider streaming, hooks, session persistence, cancellation and context preparation. - Closure path: User prompt → model answer/tool call → first-party execution/result → transcript/event append → next model decision or final answer. - Boundary reachability: AgentSession builds AgentHarness with real tool set, invokes its prompt/continue path and is launched by CLI/TUI. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model returns substantive tool choices and code; fixed argument checks and tool execution only constrain chosen actions. - Evidence: loop.py#L58-L188, harness.py#L155-L199, session.py#L680-L745. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for an installed agent/tool feedback loop. - Caveats: External live provider and coding task success were not independently run in this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party dedicated regulation of interference between distinct operational cells is established in the base coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple tool calls and user steering can interact within one session, but do not prove cross-S1 collisions or oscillation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Identify an actual conflict between separate S1 work units and feed an adjustment into their subsequent work; not supplied. - Decision owner: One agent owns its local coding sequence, deterministic runtime serializes/queues tool and user turns; no separate S2 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Single-session tool dispatch, steering/follow-up queue mode, extension lifecycle locks and local file locks. - Closure path: Model/tool events and queued user messages return to the same S1 loop, not to an S2 conflict resolution path spanning multiple S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A message queue and flexible tool/extension APIs do not by themselves implement the Methodology&#39;s distinct-S1 interference witness. - Evidence: harness.py#L31-L151, loop.py#L78-L188. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high at the one-session standard boundary. - Caveats: Separate users can run independent Baldric processes; their organizational coordination is not shipped as a Baldric S2 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current control plane over a portfolio of S1 units is evidenced at the session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/tool request limits, session cancellations, provider replacement and extension lifecycle failures are managed as single-session execution constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reallocate work, priorities, resources or commitments across multiple S1 units using a whole-system operating view; not evidenced. - Decision owner: User chooses model, session, project trust and commands; deterministic runtime enforces session and provider lifecycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxturns, model context handling, user steer/cancel, provider registry and cancellation-safe extension reload. - Closure path: Local cancellation or provider replacement affects the same coding session; no whole-system S3 control decision returns to distinct operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model switch, rate cap, process cleanup and provider lifecycle guards are not a portfolio-level managerial actor. - Evidence: harness.py#L31-L199, session.py#L680-L790. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An external team supervisor may manage many Baldric sessions but is outside this installed session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary, independently sourced challenge and corrective audit channel is established for ordinary coding outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect code and false task completion claims remain possible; ordinary tests/commands return to the same agent or user. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample code/task reality, issue a distinct challenge judgment and make it actionable through another control owner; not supplied. - Decision owner: Single coding model may run a test tool; user may inspect history; no first-party independent audit actor is configured. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool results, session history, event log, diagnostic outputs, optional extension or user-invoked test commands. - Closure path: Test output goes to the same S1 model as routine feedback; no separated audit finding with accountable returned control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Running unit tests or retaining transcripts is operational feedback, not a separate complementary audit right. - Evidence: loop.py#L112-L190, tools.py. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A manually installed reviewer extension is a possible downstream organization, not a demonstrated bundled S3 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party sustained prospective environmental sensing and adaptation-option loop into future system capability is found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New coding requirements and changed models are handled in current context or user configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Sense outside/future change, decide a durable new harness strategy and feed it into later current operation; not established. - Decision owner: User selects models, providers, prompts, skills and extensions; model makes current coding plans, not system-level future strategy revisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session persistence, skill loading, updated model catalogs, a background PyPI update notice, resource reload and user-defined custom prompts. - Closure path: A persisted transcript supplies context for resumed work; an update notice can prompt a human, but no adaptive future strategy is autonomously selected and applied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Automatically checking package versions or composing user-added skills is not model-driven S4 environmental intelligence. - Evidence: session.py#L680-L790, updatecheck.py#L135-L190, resource plan. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high at the base coding session. - Caveats: A particular user may direct Baldric to design future capabilities as an ordinary coding task, but that is not an installed S4 feedback mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime ultimate-policy/identity matter with legitimate judgment and a binding return to future operation is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Untrusted project extensions, unsafe tools, denied approvals and provider configuration inconsistencies are handled by project trust and runtime guards. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Set or revise an authoritative ultimate policy for this coding session organization after an identity-level dispute; no such loop exists. - Decision owner: User/developer sets project trust, tool eligibility and extension permissions; the runtime executes these constraints, the coding model chooses ordinary actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Project-trust prompts, extension source identity checks, tool allowlist, cancellation, provider lifecycle and session limits. - Closure path: Trust and approval choices enable or block tools; no identity-level question → authoritative parent or autonomous ruling → durable constitutional policy loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An enforced permission boundary is not by itself a VSM S5 governance body or a parent-mode closure. - Evidence: projecttrust.py, session.py#L550-L705, architecture. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer governance and installed extensions that may implement their own policy are separate recursions, not credited without concrete evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c1a746d8fe8260be58977df66bff319e2040a251/assessments/ba/baldric.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c1a746d8fe8260be58977df66bff319e2040a251\">c1a746d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:02:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kuangxing6367/gsh\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">gsh</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kuangxing6367/gsh/tree/d5d1c6160b24430b80d78f7e135bc28040faa287\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d5d1c6160b24430b80d78f7e135bc28040faa287\">d5d1c61 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Repeated model-selected workspace/remote actions in response to observed tool feedback until a final answer. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown repository or host state, tool failures, changing task details and model/tool protocol outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which registered tool to call with what arguments, interpret returned content and decide the next step or final answer. - Decision owner: The external-inference-backed agent working through gsh&#39;s first-party agentLoop; deterministic guard and human optional approval can refuse actions but do not select the task solution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin registry, JSON tool specifications, tool execution dispatcher, session JSONL, permission guard, human approval, audit log, turn cap and shell/workspace restrictions. - Closure path: User message → model Chat with tool list → model ToolCalls → registry.Execute → tool result appended as RoleTool → another model call or final answer; aborts with a bounded error after 24 rounds. - Boundary reachability: main.go and harness.Run/RunHeadless/RunREPL/RunTUI reach the same shipped Loop service. Configured OpenAI-compatible inference uses the tool-call protocol; echo is only a deterministic diagnostic mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model&#39;s proposed tool and next-turn choice determine the substantive task trajectory; the Go loop implements its feedback/action channel rather than substituting a deterministic task script. - Evidence: loop.go#L35-L137, llm.go, tools.go#L343-L405, main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for the installed agent/tool/feedback path; not a claim about model success rate. - Caveats: No live external provider was exercised here; echo is not independent evidence of autonomous model capability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No demonstrated first-party attenuation of a specific interference or oscillation between distinct autonomous S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple Web sessions and configured remote nodes may contend externally, but this distribution exposes them as sessions/execution targets, not a coordinated multi-agent S1 organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select a corrective inter-unit relation for competing S1 agents and return coordination feedback to both; no such built-in path established. - Decision owner: A model chooses tools in its own session; session IDs and Web request routing are deterministic transport. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-specific JSONL, RunSession selection, Web per-run cancellation map and shared workspace configuration. - Closure path: Each session receives its own model/tool feedback; no distinct inter-S1 conflict → coordination decision → changed future behaviour path is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Session isolation, remote-node selection and tool sequencing do not by themselves establish a function-specific S2 witness. - Evidence: loop.go#L48-L87, web/server.go#L248-L328, session.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high at the declared process/session boundary. - Caveats: A host application could compose independent agents with separate coordination, but that actor is not shipped here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate whole-system current-control discretion regulating multiple autonomous task commitments at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risk, working directory scope, concurrent Web requests and task cancellation are bounded by fixed operator/runtime rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allocate or revise current cross-cell resources, priorities, commitments or interventions based on a whole-system operating view; not wired as a distinct S3 decision path. - Decision owner: Human selects task/permission mode and individual stop; runtime applies configured guard; model decides local task steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Web session listing, current permission display, Guard.Set, per-run CancelFunc, turn cap, workspace guard and tool audit records. - Closure path: User changes a permission tier or stops an individual run and subsequent tools are permitted/blocked or cancelled; no whole-system diagnosis → current-control decision over an operational portfolio → returned cross-unit regulation is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A global permission switch and stop button are operational controls/enforcement; there is no evidenced whole-system control decision owner, autonomous or closed parent-governed, at the assessed boundary. - Evidence: web/server.go#L130-L151, web/server.go#L311-L328, web/server.go#L547-L582, perm.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium at the chosen single-process recursion. - Caveats: The Web UI exposes operator control, but the code does not establish it as a first-party S3 whole-current parent loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary operational-reality audit with corrective returned ownership is wired into the standard runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool execution errors and permission denials are logged for inspection; the same registry records ordinary events. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample reality, challenge a running unit&#39;s claim and feed an audit finding into corrective current control; not supplied. - Decision owner: Tool registry synchronously records its own execution metadata; a future human could read records but no first-party independent auditor is designated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AuditLog.Record append-only metadata, Recent, Web /api/audit and session trajectory replay. - Closure path: Registry executes/refuses a tool and logs its own result; no independent observation/verdict is automatically returned to a separate corrective control function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A file named audit and ordinary telemetry are not an S3 actor or sufficiently independent complementary-access decision. - Evidence: audit.go#L17-L113, tools.go#L343-L405, web/server.go#L508-L528. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for the distinction between logging and independent audit. - Caveats: Records might be consumed by an external auditor, which is outside this standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No separately closed external/future intelligence and strategic adaptation loop in the shipped gsh runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A model can react to new host facts inside the present task; configuration and skill discovery can change tool exposure but do not form a prospective change decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select future capability adaptations from external prospects and return them to present organizational operation; no such path is established. - Decision owner: Developer/operator configures plugins, remote nodes, providers and skill directory; model chooses current task tool actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plugin kernel, configurable tool list, on-demand skill loading, model provider switch and history replay. - Closure path: Current tool/skill info may shape subsequent task turns; no outside-and-then option generation/selection leads to an accountable later capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Plugin extensibility and local replanning are S1 execution support, not an externally prospective adaptation right. - Evidence: kernel.go, tools.go#L155-L279, skills.go, loop.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high within the pinned supported distribution. - Caveats: A consuming project might independently build evolutionary agent infrastructure on the generic kernel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and closed authoritative return path at this process recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe shell/file actions and workspace escape are checked against preselected permission/security rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Adjudicate identity-level or ultimate-policy tensions and return an authoritative change that governs later operation; not supplied. - Decision owner: Maintainer/operator establishes config and permission levels; registry applies rules. Human TUI prompt authorizes a specific risky tool, not enduring ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission guard, always-allow runtime toggles, shell safety blocklist, workspace restriction, audit and Web permission switch. - Closure path: Per-action approval or operator tier change affects subsequent tool execution; no first-party identity/policy matter reaches legitimate ultimate authority and returns as a governing policy resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed safety constraints and a user approval gate do not own an identity-level organizational discretion; no S5 parent-mode loop is wired. - Evidence: perm.go, tools.go#L299-L405, web/server.go#L560-L582. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for bounded absence. - Caveats: Operator/maintainer authority over a different organization is not part of the assessed process.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cd007e80c57e2970c862305ceb52ef09ec27904c/assessments/gs/gsh.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cd007e80c57e2970c862305ceb52ef09ec27904c\">cd007e8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T18:52:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Teraverse-AI/atoll\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Atoll</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Teraverse-AI/atoll/tree/741adc0cb28c89458815588d4418afe9336adcbf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 741adc0cb28c89458815588d4418afe9336adcbf\">741adc0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: One model-driven task agent edits/reads accounting workspace artifacts, uses a published tool surface and can adjust its next action after tool outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task differences, spreadsheet/PDF/file state, tool errors, model context and failed/no-result calls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured inference model chooses which tool calls to emit, with what arguments, whether to continue or finalize, after receiving prior tool results. - Decision owner: Configured task agent model through Atoll&#39;s installed LoopTruncatedToolsAgent; NativeToolProvider executes rather than deciding task strategy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Native tool registry/schema, provider adapter, context-trim notices, refusal/spill on tool-result budget, max-steps/timeout/token-cost bounds, code sandbox and trajectory records. - Closure path: Task package → CLI workspace creation → model call with native tool schemas → selected tool execution → text/error/image observation → next model step/final answer → persisted workspace/result. - Boundary reachability: The CLI actually instantiates the native provider and loop and runs them, with configured real model provider option. Shipped code was adapted from Mercor, but this repo installs and executes it locally; credit is for Atoll-distributed reachability, not original algorithm ownership. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model discretionary tool choice and observation-conditioned continuation make S1=A. Host budget enforcement, externally supplied model backend and fixture grader do not own those choices. - Evidence: entry, loop, native provider. - Basis: Installed first-party runtime wiring and model/tool/result continuation; imported provenance explicitly bounded. - Confidence: High for source reachability; no configured real-provider E2E exercised locally. - Caveats: Some native tool families are documented placeholders and Docker is required for code execution. E2E task quality and comparative scores are not claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independent task-operating S1 work units are mutually coordinated within one Atoll run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent tool calls and task input/workspace conflicts are possible but handled as one model-run sequence, not observed cross-S1 policy attenuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A single model initiates tool calls; native provider dispatches them; operator selects mounted tool families. - Decision owner: Same task model for local steps and deterministic provider/CLI for routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Namespaced native tool schemas, tool provider registry, per-step batch results and workspace isolation. - Closure path: Model tool batch → provider results → same model. No two S1 units → an inter-unit conflict signal → S2 balancing authority → revised operation in both. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Many tools or parallel tool calls are resources of one S1 agent, not two independently deciding agents requiring coordination. - Evidence: loop, provider, agent README. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external benchmark farm could orchestrate many independent runs; that operation is outside the published per-run system boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-portfolio present management chooses priorities, budgets or corrective action for distinct S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model time, token/cost budget, run errors, context-window exhaustion and overlarge tool results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator-provided caps and deterministic code warn/stop/truncate/spill one run; they do not allocate a current operating portfolio autonomously. - Decision owner: Benchmark config/CLI operator and predefined local loop conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxsteps, timeout, cost/token budget warnings, result budgeting, status output and per-run metrics. - Closure path: Budget threshold → warning/final turn or failure; no whole-system resource-monitoring authority → reprioritization → multiple S1 behavioural corrections. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Rich telemetry and deterministic per-run cap enforcement are local S1 constraints, not a separate whole-current S3 manager. - Evidence: main config, loop budgets, agent docs. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Externally operated scheduler or grading engine could use these outputs for decisions; none is installed as controlling authority here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent audit checks the real agent output using a separate evidence channel and returns findings to management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Errors in final workbooks, task completion or model claims could be assessed through snapshots/gold answer, but the published verifier/grading engine is explicitly deferred. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Runner captures initial/final snapshots and trajectory; no implemented verifier renders an independent correctness verdict during the deployed workflow. - Decision owner: None for installed S3; potential future grading code, human evaluator or benchmark operator lies outside the executing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pre/post workspace snapshots, JSONL trajectory, sample goldanswer.json and verifier schema definitions; no live scoring feedback path. - Closure path: Agent run → saved snapshots/results → possible future offline evaluation; missing independent audit observation, verdict and control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Evidence artifacts are inputs an auditor might use; their existence, and tests against fixtures, do not itself constitute an independent complementary audit function. - Evidence: grading deferred, runner output, examples. - Basis: Explicit absent implementation and bounded first-party return path. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The project aims for Archipelago-compatible benchmarking, but future plans and compatibility claims are not evidence of a deployed S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No prospective external-environment intelligence selects future operating adaptations for the harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future provider changes, tool-family availability, model context budgets and benchmark task design. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator configures task/model/tools and developers update vendor ports; the model addresses its assigned current task. - Decision owner: Human evaluation operator or maintainer for future changes; not an installed autonomous future-management function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task TOML config, model adapters, optional future gateway provider stub, response-surgery compatibility fixes and manual upstream resync. - Closure path: Current task → present tool work/result; code/config update → next run when human changes it. No prospective environmental signal → autonomous adaptation policy → adopted new capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Provider compatibility retries and vendored resync are tactical/repository-level mechanisms, not agency choosing a future viable operating model. - Evidence: agent docs, runner, Atoll fixes. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Model may plan long-horizon steps within a finite task; that is not separately installed organizational S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct identity/ultimate policy decision authority closes a mandate back into the agent&#39;s operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Benchmark validity, sandbox safety, tool access and financial-workspace data boundaries. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator supplies task, prompt and network/tool safety configuration; static code limits execution and writes artifacts. - Decision owner: Human deployment maintainer/evaluation operator; not an installed S5 body or recognized parent-assisted policy cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Static accounting assistant prompt, task TOML, per-run sandbox/network boundary and repository license/NOTICE. - Closure path: External setup rules → current run constraints; no agent identity-level issue → legitimate policy authority judgment → returned organizational mandate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Safety isolation and license compliance are essential but do not constitute agent-owned ultimate governance or a closed parent S5 decision right. - Evidence: runner prompt, readme, environment docs. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The project developer controls product identity, but their repository decisions are outside the observed task runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/424e48ecd9ca99ab44953f3b91466b726e721604/assessments/at/atoll.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 424e48ecd9ca99ab44953f3b91466b726e721604\">424e48e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:31:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dream-x/mark2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mark2</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/dream-x/mark2/tree/535ffc4710e4583b5657dea94bc970d9d2c05bc3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 535ffc4710e4583b5657dea94bc970d9d2c05bc3\">535ffc4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: coding work through a model-directed observation/action feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file contents, command results, malformed arguments and denied actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next workspace action or completion from returned observations. - Decision owner: the model-backed agent in the first-party core. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider streaming, ToolRegistry validation/execution, PermissionPolicy, cancellation and SessionRecorder. - Closure path: UserInput → model tool-use → permission/validation/execution → ToolResult in history → next model decision; no tool-use ends the turn. - Boundary reachability: both shipped CLI modes construct the core Agent and consume its events; Yolo permits the standard tool loop, and supervised modes retain model action choice with human permission constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model leaves no actor choosing how to solve the task; remaining code only applies preselected guards and executes selected tools. - Evidence: crates/core/src/agent.rs; crates/core/src/tools/mod.rs; crates/core/src/permission.rs; crates/cli/src/main.rs. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-level reachable ownership, not a new live-model success measurement or a claim that all tool effects are autonomous in AskWrites.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party function-specific path established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the nearest examined mechanisms concern one session serializes its own tool calls; no distinct shipped S1 cells interact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-level right is exposed by the examined distribution. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner; task-local model choices and developer/user settings retain their narrower scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent loop, sequential tools and library/CLI entry points. - Closure path: no function-specific returned closure; ordinary coding feedback is already S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Sequential tools, repeated-call suppression and write-churn feedback stabilize one S1, not inter-unit interference. Running multiple independent processes is possible but no shipped cross-session coordination relation closes their conflicts. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md; crates/core/src/agent.rs; crates/core/src/tools/mod.rs. - Basis: structural bounded absence after positive and eligible parent-mode checks. - Confidence: high for the declared product boundary. - Caveats: a custom external controller could add this function; that hypothetical extension is outside this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party function-specific path established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the nearest examined mechanisms concern task/session execution guards rather than a whole-system commitment regulator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-level right is exposed by the examined distribution. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner; task-local model choices and developer/user settings retain their narrower scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxturns, cancellation, permission approval, context thresholds and CLI configuration. - Closure path: no function-specific returned closure; ordinary coding feedback is already S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The examined controls enforce user/developer choices over one coding turn. Neither an autonomous current-control actor nor a parent-facing whole-system observation → commitment/resource decision → returned intervention mode is supplied. Generic library operations do not constitute an S3-specific constructor. - Evidence: crates/core/src/agent.rs; crates/core/src/permission.rs; crates/core/src/config.rs; crates/cli/src/interactive.rs. - Basis: structural bounded absence after positive and eligible parent-mode checks. - Confidence: high for the declared product boundary. - Caveats: a custom external controller could add this function; that hypothetical extension is outside this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party function-specific path established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the nearest examined mechanisms concern ordinary tool feedback and adjacent benchmark checking. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-level right is exposed by the examined distribution. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner; task-local model choices and developer/user settings retain their narrower scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SessionRecorder, ToolResult errors, write-churn nudges, unit/e2e tests and bench crate boundary. - Closure path: no function-specific returned closure; ordinary coding feedback is already S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model sees its ordinary execution feedback. No separate actor obtains complementary raw evidence to challenge an operational completion claim and return findings into control; the benchmark is outside the installed product loop. - Evidence: crates/core/src/session.rs; crates/core/src/agent.rs; crates/bench/src/runner.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: structural bounded absence after positive and eligible parent-mode checks. - Confidence: high for the declared product boundary. - Caveats: a custom external controller could add this function; that hypothetical extension is outside this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party function-specific path established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the nearest examined mechanisms concern context retention and task-local repair. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-level right is exposed by the examined distribution. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner; task-local model choices and developer/user settings retain their narrower scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: file-memory loading/writing, compaction, provider configuration and research/benchmark separation. - Closure path: no function-specific returned closure; ordinary coding feedback is already S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory and summaries preserve operational context. Choosing the next coding action or a configured model is not prospective environment-facing adaptation of the harness. No supported autonomous or parent-governed external sensing → adaptation options → changed present capability loop is supplied. - Evidence: crates/core/src/memory.rs; crates/core/src/prompt.rs; crates/core/src/compact.rs; crates/core/src/config.rs; ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: structural bounded absence after positive and eligible parent-mode checks. - Confidence: high for the declared product boundary. - Caveats: a custom external controller could add this function; that hypothetical extension is outside this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party function-specific path established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the nearest examined mechanisms concern static prompt and permissions plus ordinary tool approval. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying function-level right is exposed by the examined distribution. - Decision owner: no qualifying owner; task-local model choices and developer/user settings retain their narrower scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project AGENTS.md loading, system prompt, Yolo/AskWrites/ReadOnly policies and Approve/Interrupt operations. - Closure path: no function-specific returned closure; ordinary coding feedback is already S1. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime applies preselected instructions and individual action decisions; it supplies no identity/ultimate-policy issue submission and authoritative returned decision governing later operation. File editability and ordinary source maintenance do not establish a packaged parent governance mode. - Evidence: crates/core/src/prompt.rs; crates/core/src/permission.rs; crates/core/src/event.rs; crates/cli/src/interactive.rs. - Basis: structural bounded absence after positive and eligible parent-mode checks. - Confidence: high for the declared product boundary. - Caveats: a custom external controller could add this function; that hypothetical extension is outside this assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8cb60aa0831c652a6f7980ea4338c784452c7fe4/assessments/ma/mark2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8cb60aa0831c652a6f7980ea4338c784452c7fe4\">8cb60aa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T18:42:23+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/shim\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shim</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/shim/tree/22b485fe5ac516f8d1ae44de2edeb77e907cfe9e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 22b485fe5ac516f8d1ae44de2edeb77e907cfe9e\">22b485f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed workspace task work with tool observations fed into subsequent decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, changing workspace files and shell outputs, MCP/tool errors and model-generated tool arguments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured model selects ordinary response versus tool calls, target tool and arguments and next response after receiving observations. - Decision owner: Configured model through first-party agent.Agent.Run, with host execution mediated by the installed tool executor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider normalization, tool schema registry, bounded MaxSteps, workspace path checks, read-only/ask/auto permission gate and append-only session history. - Closure path: Terminal prompt → CLI composition → provider inference → model-selected tool → executor/workspace or MCP → tool result message → next inference or final response. - Boundary reachability: runPrompt registers tools and calls the agent loop; real provider clients are explicitly configurable. It is more than a test-only agent skeleton, although remote-provider operation was not replayed here. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses task steps and reacts to tool outcomes. Shell/permission transports and human-approval prompts do not themselves choose strategy. - Evidence: run wiring, agent loop, executor, MCP. - Basis: Installed first-party decision/action/observation/redecision route evidenced by source and unit-test scaffolding. - Confidence: High for source reachability; not a claim of an executed paid-provider E2E. - Caveats: The chosen external model&#39;s behavior and any MCP server are outside first-party Shim; permissions may stop tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No intra-recursion coordination among distinct autonomously task-operating S1 units is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool name collisions, concurrent model-provider options and MCP availability are possible technical conflicts, not demonstrated conflict between autonomous S1 work units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The registry rejects collisions and a human selects a model; no agent resolves clashes between S1 objectives. - Decision owner: Static namespace/registry and operator configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, MCP namespacing, CLI model selection and management router allowlist. - Closure path: Registry collision → setup failure, or user configuration → next session; no two S1 cells → coordination decision → returned operational adjustment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool schemas and MCP endpoints are services consumed by one model, not multiple independently deciding S1 agents. - Evidence: MCP manager, registry, run. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: User-authored external MCP services could implement coordination, but are outside this first-party boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No present-time portfolio control of independently operating S1 units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Excess steps, risky file changes and tool failures are bounded at one agent&#39;s task level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: MaxSteps errors after a fixed number of turns; permission mode is configured by a human and can request one-shot approval, not autonomously reallocate commitments. - Decision owner: CLI operator and static loop/executor conditions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Step cap, --read-only/--yes, executor mode and REPL confirmations, session logging. - Closure path: Limit or denied tool call → current-run error/result; there is no S3 observation/priority/resource decision affecting a portfolio of S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Host guardrails regulate one S1&#39;s conduct without independently managing whole-system current performance. - Evidence: agent loop, executor, CLI run. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Per-task model self-monitoring belongs within S1 at this recursion, not an independent S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separately owned independent reality-checking audit lane complements current S3 management. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Malicious tool outputs, unsafe filesystem changes and provider replies may be constrained by prompt or tool gates, not independently audited. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Executor checks static level and human permission; unit tests use fixtures, neither yields independent runtime audit findings returned to S3. - Decision owner: Operator for approvals, deterministic guards for execution, developer for tests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission gating, resolved-path confinement, agent prompt treating tool/MCP content as untrusted, unit tests. - Closure path: Tool response returns to same model or approval blocks one operation; no alternate evidence acquisition → independent audit verdict → corrective S3 management path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human approval and ordinary unit tests are not an installed S3 auditor, and agent self-recheck would not be independent. - Evidence: executor, prompt, tool tests. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Operators may run external audits; this is not a first-party installed runtime audit channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No first-party prospective environmental strategy/adaptation cycle is installed over the harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Model/provider availability, new MCP services, skill updates and evolving user tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operator selects/adds providers, skills or MCP services; a compiled management command is whitelisted and explicitly confirmed before mutating. - Decision owner: Human operator; narrow router compiler can suggest a bounded command but has no independent future-horizon mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Config updates, model discovery, lazy skill loading, MCP registration and opt-in model-based router. - Closure path: Manual configuration change or task reply → present operation; no future-environment intelligence → strategy decision → coordination with S3 → adopted future change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: CLI convenience for service installation and slash-command compilation is not owned prospective strategy or adaptation feedback. - Evidence: router, REPL, architecture. - Basis: Bounded absence review including strongest S4 candidate, the optional AI management router. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Individual agent may discuss future plans as ordinary S1 user work; no installed organizational S4 is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No distinct ultimate identity-policy authority and closed mandate governs the running agent organizationally. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User intent, workspace permissions, untrusted instructions and changing local configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The human sets permissions/model and confirms mutating router requests; static prompt tells the model to treat external content as untrusted. - Decision owner: External operator/developer for policy, not a packaged S5 governance role or agent-level identity body. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prompt safety text, local AGENTS.md injection, tool approval/read-only restrictions, router whitelist. - Closure path: Human config/approval → a permitted task action; no identity-level issue → legitimate ultimate-policy deliberation or parent decision → returned enforceable organizational policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static instructions, user approvals and optional AGENTS.md do not prove S5 self-governance or an accountable parent-assisted policy cycle. - Evidence: prompt construction, executor, REPL. - Basis: Bounded absence review. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Human policy ownership exists outside this assessed local runtime, but the specific returned S5 control path is not installed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e4e903f468ba1b87614c7a6a6b167caaccfedf5c/assessments/sh/shim.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e4e903f468ba1b87614c7a6a6b167caaccfedf5c\">e4e903f &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:22:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samkerrcode/opengent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenGent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/samkerrcode/opengent/tree/f981d42614d49bda064c474587095a49a0c2bb28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f981d42614d49bda064c474587095a49a0c2bb28\">f981d42 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-led local task execution with tool-result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool failures, unanticipated responses, model/provider errors, incomplete tasks and user-selected access constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next tool/action or conclude the task after seeing prior results. - Decision owner: Configured model-driven chat agent, with human ownership of optional individual approvals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust RunCoordinator, MCP tool catalog, dispatch policy, approval timeouts, cancellation, context limits and SQLite persistence. - Closure path: Executed/denied tool results enter the conversation and can change the next model step; completed turn reaches a terminal response. - Boundary reachability: Shipped RunCoordinator::start and runloop wire provider stream into actual executecall/tool results; this is not a test-only mocked workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model discretion the host still enforces policies but cannot choose the task-sensitive sequence of actions. - Evidence: coordinator.rs#L305, coordinator.rs#L491, agent/mod.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the source-visible model/tool loop, not live task success. - Caveats: A real provider and user-authorized MCP tools must be supplied; no provider task run was executed for this review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulation of interference between distinct S1 operational units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential simultaneous runs/shared tools and per-chat overlap checked within the desktop boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate inter-S1 conflict-adjustment decision was found. - Decision owner: No S2-specific agent/constructor; host serialization and user configuration remain mechanisms. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-chat exclusivity slots, cancellation and stored scheduled jobs. - Closure path: No demonstrated feedback relating one S1&#39;s interference to adjustments in another S1&#39;s subsequent behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A mutex on the same chat and a schedule queue do not make a multi-unit conflict regulator. - Evidence: coordinator.rs#L78, schedule.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: External users can coordinate multiple chat agents themselves.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Current whole-application allocation/commitment regulation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent workload, model fallback, timeouts and permission limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped whole-system discretionary prioritization or corrective commitment right evidenced. - Decision owner: User-selected schedule/configuration and deterministic local run controller, not a dedicated S3 agent or parent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run slots, fallback on provider error, step caps, per-tool approval and stored schedules. - Closure path: Individual run enforcement/fallback closes locally without an app-wide view→decision→reallocation return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard limits and operator-set priorities do not exercise whole-system managerial discretion. - Evidence: coordinator.rs#L305, storage/agents.rs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human management outside the app is not a first-party closed S3 parent mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independent challenge to routine operational claims using complementary access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect but convincing agent outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent audit judgment with returned corrective authority is shipped. - Decision owner: User can inspect visible traces; ordinary runtime records events, but neither is an independent S3 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Streamed tool events, persisted transcripts, simulated-provider/CI tests and user approval cards. - Closure path: No alternate outcome evidence→independent verdict→control correction path beyond the same run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transparent traces and routine tool-result checking repeat ordinary reporting, not independent audit. - Evidence: agent/recorder.rs, domain/events.rs, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A downstream reviewer can audit exports, but no first-party independent corrective path was established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/future-oriented adaptation of the harness&#39;s own capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider/tool evolution, changing user needs and future capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party prospective adaptation decision returned to current capability. - Decision owner: Developer/desktop user configures providers/tools and roadmap; task agent does not own capability evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider registry, saved agents, on-demand MCP discovery, fallback models and schedules. - Closure path: Configuration changes require user/developer selection; local task feedback does not establish prospective organizational adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Selecting a tool for the current task differs from researching future environment changes and installing a revised repertoire. - Evidence: providers/registry.rs, agent/tools.rs, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A future plugin/application could add adaptation, outside the reviewed distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity and ultimate-policy resolution for the application. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential changes to the product&#39;s governing purpose versus ordinary tool risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level matter→legitimate decision→returned policy closure is implemented. - Decision owner: Maintainer and desktop user set policies outside an S5 runtime path; tool approval is task-local. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool policy modes, per-call approval, secrets, static product configuration and user-defined agent instructions. - Closure path: Tool grants/denials feed one tool request or persistent access settings, not a returned identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even hard permission enforcement cannot determine the identity/ultimate policy of the system. - Evidence: mcp/permissions.rs, coordinator.rs#L587, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: The repository may have human maintainer governance, distinct from the installed app recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/85d0bf17e9f13fa4225d00151865719918b79c6a/assessments/op/opengent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 85d0bf17e9f13fa4225d00151865719918b79c6a\">85d0bf1 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T20:37:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nogo/faden\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">faden</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nogo/faden/tree/e59833d32c775b86c7b35b2ca1b3cd1b042b5886\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e59833d32c775b86c7b35b2ca1b3cd1b042b5886\">e59833d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute a user request through a first-party model/tool decision-feedback loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown task steps, returned tool data, model/tool errors and interrupted progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool calls, arguments and next model action after observing outcomes. - Decision owner: The configured model operating through faden&#39;s own llama.Backend tool loop (and alternatively existing external agents through adapters, without claiming their internals). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run/complete/execute, host-registered Go tools, durable transcript events and replay safety controls. - Closure path: Host Submit → persisted turn → llama completion selects tool → faden records/executes result → model sees returned result and chooses next step or final answer → persisted completion. - Boundary reachability: llama is a shipped first-party package with New/Run, shown in the documented quick-start and wired through standard faden.Open backends; no external coding-agent SDK must furnish the loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects actions and interprets tool outputs; event persistence, oldest-first queue and crash retry merely keep that operation durable. - Evidence: llama/llama.go, Run and execute, llama/tools.go, README.md, quick start. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for native shipped loop; no live model server execution in this review. - Caveats: Host selects backend/model, workspace and actual function permissions. Model intelligence is external, but tool loop and persistence are first-party.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced distinct inter-S1 mutual adjustment within the first-party runner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate request IDs and competing turns in one session are prevented by deterministic serialization, not agent mediation of distinct workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Interpret collisions between separate agents and change their reciprocal task commitments; absent. - Decision owner: Host selects backend; Runner locks and orders submissions mechanically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-session active-turn map, request-ID idempotency, FIFO turn selection and backend cursor handover. - Closure path: Runner serializes a single session and preserves events; no inter-agent conflict finding is interpreted by an S2 decision owner and returned to S1 peers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A session switching sequentially between backends is not two simultaneously interacting S1 units; locks and ordering alone are not S2 autonomy. - Evidence: runner.go, README.md, handover/handover.go. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high at declared session recursion. - Caveats: Host apps could build their own multi-agent coordination above faden; it is not in this boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No separate agent-owned whole-system current resource/commitment management is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Restarted turns, queue backlogs and backend availability are runtime failure/recovery signals, not managed by a whole-system model policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reassign priorities, budgets and multiple operational units from an integrated current view; absent. - Decision owner: Host app names the backend, input and workspace; Runner deterministically processes one turn per session and bounded attempts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Open/Submit, store cursor and attempts, Drain/Wait, max attempts and context selection. - Closure path: Process restart → Runner reloads events and resumes the same assigned task, without deciding organizational commitments or allocating new priorities. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Resilient scheduling is significant infrastructure but not a substantive whole-system agent decision right; S1 continuity does not imply S3. - Evidence: runner.go, faden.go, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for reviewed first-party scheduler/runner. - Caveats: A host could create an agentic supervisor, but the public faden API does not attribute that supervisor to faden.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently owned claim audit/correction path across ordinary agent reports and underlying evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing tool results or interrupted turns are reconstructed for continuity rather than treated as potentially false operational claims to independently check. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently inspect the underlying artifact, challenge agent completion and return an enforceable correction; absent. - Decision owner: Model continues its own turn; host may inspect store events, but no separate auditor is allocated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stored event transcripts, tool pre/post events, crash-safe replay, test fixtures and readthread. - Closure path: Replayed event history returns to the original/next agent for work continuity; no independent reviewer judgment → rejected claim → corrective S1 path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Auditable event logs are evidence access, not a first-party model/auditor with a mandate and independent return channel. - Evidence: llama/llama.go, attempt.go, handover/handover.go. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Event logs make external audits possible but the harness does not assign the S3 task to an independent decision owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No distinct externally prospective capability-learning/adaptation owner is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Backend switching, loss of tool context and restart state are present task-continuity disturbances. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Generate, select and enact future organizational capability options from external developments; absent. - Decision owner: Host preselects backend and model per turn; faden creates bounded catch-up from past events mechanically. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Handover cursors, budgeted context selection, prior transcript retention and library backend injection. - Closure path: Historic messages → context slice → next model turn; no future-oriented proposal/evaluation/authorized persistent capability change is returned. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Moving an active task from Claude to llama is host-selected execution continuity, not prospective environmental adaptation. - Evidence: handover/handover.go, runner.go, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Host applications can learn from transcripts; faden itself does not implement their future strategy.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No built-in ultimate-purpose/policy governance or legitimate parent constitutional adjudication is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool side effects, host credentials, retention and retry limits, not identity/policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authoritatively change binding purpose or resolve a contested ultimate policy and return it to operations; absent. - Decision owner: Host controls tools, workspace, models, credentials, permissions and overall user intent; faden only enforces local safe-replay boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Host-configured Tool.Safe, runtime setup, max attempts, failed-turn status and passed-in system instructions. - Closure path: Unsafe interrupted tool is not automatically retried; status returns to host. No policy conflict is decided at a legitimate internal/parent authority and carried back to the agent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic safety and error outcomes do not constitute S5 purpose decision rights. - Evidence: llama/llama.go, runner.go, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Different host organizations may have external S5 controls, beyond the assessed library boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/1738db4ae8d06d7eee28fcb4eed3681a374ac23d/assessments/fa/faden.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 1738db4ae8d06d7eee28fcb4eed3681a374ac23d\">1738db4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T18:36:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/uncut\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNCUT</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Balanov/uncut/tree/e1512a702de846eb701e0383d9ae031bad528c0f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e1512a702de846eb701e0383d9ae031bad528c0f\">e1512a7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete a user-requested coding/investigation outcome through a model-chosen sequence of workspace actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown source organization, edit/test outcomes, command failures and incomplete information discovered while implementing the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and revise file/shell operations from returned evidence, decide whether more work is needed, and issue the final result. - Decision owner: the configured model-backed coding agent in Runner.Run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Go call loop, four tool implementations, workspace path guard, provider adapter, session log, step budget, timeout and output-error handling. - Closure path: task prompt → model tool call → Executor.Execute → tool result appended to message history → next model judgment/action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: the standard uncut CLI constructs Runner with provider and executor and invokes Runner.Run; no dogfood-only or sample integration is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: with the model removed the runtime can expose tools and enforce limits but cannot select substantive edits or interpret feedback into task completion. - Evidence: main.go, run.go, tools.go, runtest.go. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the wired model/tool feedback path; live provider task success is not claimed. - Caveats: model quality and credentials are external; the shell can act broadly within OS permissions despite the file-tool workspace check.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party coordination among distinct operating S1 units is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no shipped peer-agent interference or oscillation is identified at this single-agent boundary. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 adjustment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential tool-call execution and one shared conversation/session. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing multiple tool calls from one model is not the coordination of distinct autonomous S1 units. - Evidence: run.go, types.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external shell commands could launch independent agents, but UNCUT does not ship and coordinate such a peer organization.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system supervisory current-control loop exists above the one S1 agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-run step/time limits and tool errors are bounded locally, without whole-system resource/commitment management. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for substantive cross-unit current control. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: --max-steps, HTTP timeout, context cancellation and error exits. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator preselects limits and the runtime enforces them; the coding agent does not gain a whole-system live view or authority to reallocate independent units&#39; commitments. - Evidence: main.go, run.go. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a single S1 agent can choose its next local action; that is not by itself a higher-recursion S3 decision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit channel is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file/test evidence may be inspected by the ordinary coding agent, but no separate complementary audit challenges its reporting. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an independent audit judgment. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: readfile, runcommand, full tool-result logging and optional NDJSON observation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the same S1 model may run tests or read diffs; these ordinary feedback operations and emitted logs do not supply an independent auditor and corrective-return decision. - Evidence: tools.go, run.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: an external operator can audit emitted events or instruct a separate reviewer, but such an auditor is not a shipped first-party organizational path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changes in future tools/models/environment are left to external operator choices, rather than modeled by a first-party capability-adaptation process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for future-oriented adaptation options. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reusable session messages, configurable provider URL/model, system instructions and AGENTS.md loading. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conversation continuation, current-task replanning and manual provider selection do not develop externally grounded future options and return them into durable present capability. - Evidence: main.go, session.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a user could ask for strategic research as an ordinary task, but the standard harness lacks a continuing S4 feedback mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is closed at the running harness boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operating instructions and tool scope constrain the coding task, but identity-level conflict is not routed to authoritative governance and returned. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at ultimate-policy level. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static default system prompt, optional workspace AGENTS.md, CLI choices, file path validation and hard limits. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator sets instructions and limits, the Go runtime enforces tool semantics, and no shipped identity-policy proposal/decision/return route exists; a generic human task instruction is not S5 parent closure. - Evidence: main.go, tools.go, README. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: repository-maintainer policy/release decisions belong to an adjacent OSS development organization rather than this CLI session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e97a86e2c5b160b500b66d88d5fc92f524433c62/assessments/un/uncut.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e97a86e2c5b160b500b66d88d5fc92f524433c62\">e97a86e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:38:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ytinyx/astra-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Astra Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ytinyx/astra-harness/tree/0c24783eaef9d3b00f7176eb86b75410f42231a2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c24783eaef9d3b00f7176eb86b75410f42231a2\">0c24783 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: repository-owned model/tool iterative task actor transforms goals into tool-backed outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task requirements, tool observations/errors, approval decisions, uncertain side effects and workspace state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next task-relevant tool action or final response based on prior messages and tool results. - Decision owner: model-guided task agent as wired by Engine.model and tool within the first-party runtime; provider computes outputs but is not a foreign agent harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool schema/permissions, time/step budgets, approval hash, run store, workspace/model bindings, cancel/pause/unknown-state guards. - Closure path: submitted task → model response → validation and tool call or explicit approval → result/error appended to conversation → subsequent model decision/final result. - Boundary reachability: Application.submit creates and persists an Engine state, worker advances it, and the shipped CLI/web APIs expose the complete local mode; no example-only actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model outputs choose task actions; host caps, queues and permissions enforce externally selected constraints instead of choosing the solution. - Evidence: engine, application. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-controlled and local test evidence; no claim of paid-provider real-world task success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no independently established inter-S1 adjustment/anti-oscillation between distinct operative agent units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active-session duplication, request-id collisions and uncertain side effects are controlled at the run-store/executor level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose coordination to attenuate interference among multiple S1 units; no such agentic cross-unit choice is shipped. - Decision owner: deterministic Application admission and its single worker, not a separate S2 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: requestid idempotence/conflict checks, one-active-run-per-session check, single worker, blocking unknown states and tool-update reservations. - Closure path: invalid/conflicting submissions are rejected or queued; a single run executes at a time, rather than negotiating/feedback-adjusting two simultaneous S1 agent behaviours. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: meaningful safety isolation exists, but it is a serialized host execution policy, not evidenced S2-specific coordination between independently operating first-party S1 units. - Evidence: application. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a multi-agent composition could reuse these primitives, but is not the assessed standard mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independent whole-system current-operational supervisory decision over an S1 organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool risk, runtime limits, queue state, model and workspace configuration drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/revise shared operational commitments, priorities and resources for the whole. - Decision owner: local operator for approvals/configuration, runtime for deterministic safety; model actor owns task-local actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: statuses, limits, select-profile/workspace API, pause/cancel, reservetoolupdate, SQLite run views. - Closure path: per-tool approval/denial or config reservation changes one run&#39;s feasibility; no function-specific parent supervisory whole-system decision returns to a distinct current S1 organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an operator dashboard and execution controls alone do not establish S3, S3=P or autonomous current-control ownership. - Evidence: application, engine. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a larger multi-user organization would require a new boundary review.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no operational independent audit of routine execution claims plus corrective return is wired into the user task mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: invalid model responses, unsupported tools, uncertain effects and model/time budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect actual operation and challenge ordinary reporting to control. - Decision owner: no separate audit actor; executor/host validators perform regular checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event tracing, snapshots, protocol validators, unknown-effect quarantine, tests and benchmark evaluator. - Closure path: a failed check or uncertain side effect stops/blocks a run; benchmark and tests are external development evidence, not a deployed complementary auditor returning corrective management feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source-level integrity checks are valuable but not a functionally separate independent S3 relationship. - Evidence: engine, evaluation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external operator audits could be added but are not first-party active paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no future-oriented environmental options loop closes into current operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user prompts, added knowledge materials, model profile choices and extension configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret external/future distinctions, generate adaptation options and return changes to present capabilities. - Decision owner: local user/operator selects profiles, workspaces and knowledge; task model handles immediate goals. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/skill/knowledge profiles, context assembly, extension tooling and workbench APIs. - Closure path: selected content/configuration conditions later model requests, but no standing prospective alternative-evaluation/selection/implementation path is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: contextual knowledge and configurable tools are not S4 without external-and-prospective adaptation feedback. - Evidence: application, knowledge library. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external product development and offline benchmarking are adjacent, not shipped S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no autonomous or operational parent-governed ultimate-policy/identity decision path exists at the assessed local task recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, credential protection, workspace selection and tool-approval rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle identity/ultimate-policy matters and bind future operation. - Decision owner: developer/operator configuration and ordinary action-specific human approval, not a demonstrated ultimate-policy authority/return loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable tool snapshots, explicit approvals, permission catalogs, system instruction and host security boundaries. - Closure path: updated configuration or user grant constrains later tool calls; no identity-level matter-to-legitimate-parent decision and returned governance transition is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: security controls and per-tool approvals are not S5 or S5=P merely because a human can override a run. - Evidence: engine, application. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: wider institutional deployment governance is outside this local single-user boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/83a99f0f9355a5f4038235f19c1856b2b3d57439/assessments/as/astra-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 83a99f0f9355a5f4038235f19c1856b2b3d57439\">83a99f0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
